/* Consolidated IFDSM Volume I: 10 chapters, 78 outcomes, exact SBA links and updated PDF pages. */
window.IFDSM_CHAPTERS=[{"id":1,"title":"Normal sleep, sleep disorders and pathophysiology of SDB","short":"Normal sleep and SDB mechanisms","lens":"Recognise sleep-disordered breathing, distinguish disease mechanisms, and explain why a dental intervention will or will not work.","sections":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Benefits and functions of normal sleep","text":"Normal sleep supports cognition, mood, immune regulation and metabolic homeostasis. When a patient reports fatigue, determine whether the problem is insufficient sleep, fragmented sleep, insomnia, or respiratory events rather than assuming OSA.","question_ids":[32,34,52,55,68,73,78,85],"examples":[{"id":32,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: declarative in NREM (sleep spindles), procedural and emotional in REM. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":16},{"id":34,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about memory consolidation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":17},{"id":52,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: synaptic and neuronal function; glial-mediated glymphatic clearance of metabolites; glycogen restoration; neurogenesis. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":24},{"id":55,"stem":"Which of the following statements about biochemical refreshment is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":25},{"id":68,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: growth hormone in early SWS; prolactin, testosterone, PTH; thyroid hormone late evening; melatonin under light–dark control. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":31},{"id":73,"stem":"Which of the following statements about hormone regulation is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":32},{"id":78,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: REM-dependent processing of affect. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":34},{"id":85,"stem":"Which of the following statements about emotional regulation is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":37}],"count":8,"pdf_page":9},{"id":"1.2","title":"Sleep physiology and architecture","text":"NREM and REM are distinct physiological states. REM-related muscle atonia can aggravate upper-airway obstruction; a study that captures little REM may not represent the full clinical burden.","question_ids":[3,7,8,15,35,37,54,57,80,87,90,97,98,105],"examples":[{"id":3,"stem":"A shift worker sleeps for eight hours before a night shift but becomes sleepy at 04:00. Which mechanism best explains the sleepiness?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":6},{"id":7,"stem":"Which of the following statements about REM atonia applies to the pharyngeal dilators too is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":8},{"id":8,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: This is why REM-predominant OSA exists and why it responds relatively poorly to mandibular advancement . Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":8},{"id":15,"stem":"An adult's overnight study shows REM periods lengthening towards morning. What is the best physiological explanation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":10},{"id":35,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: homeostatic sleep drive. Sleep pressure accumulates with time awake (adenosine accumulation is the principal candidate mediator) and dissipates during sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":17},{"id":37,"stem":"Which of the following statements about process s is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":18},{"id":54,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: GABA and galanin from the ventrolateral preoptic area. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":24},{"id":57,"stem":"Which of the following statements about NREM-promoting is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":25},{"id":80,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: orexin/hypocretin (lateral hypothalamus), noradrenaline (locus coeruleus), serotonin (raphe), histamine (tuberomammillary), acetylcholine, dopamine. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":35},{"id":87,"stem":"Which of the following statements about wake-promoting is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":38},{"id":90,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: ~13–23%, 20–40 min first cycle. High-voltage slow delta; highest arousal threshold of all NREM stages; concentrated in the first third of the night; GH pulse here; disorders of arousal arise here. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":39},{"id":97,"stem":"Which of the following statements about n3 (old stages 3+4) is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":41},{"id":98,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: endogenous ~24 h oscillator in the suprachiasmatic nucleus, entrained by light via the retinohypothalamic tract. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":42},{"id":105,"stem":"Which of the following statements about process c (circadian) is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":44}],"count":14,"pdf_page":10},{"id":"1.3","title":"Anatomical/non-anatomical SDB mechanisms","text":"Separate anatomy (airway size and collapsibility/Pcrit) from non-anatomical traits (loop gain, arousal threshold, dilator-muscle responsiveness). A mandibular advancement device mainly addresses the anatomical component, not every ventilatory-control abnormality.","question_ids":[5,10,11,18,21,24,38,40,42,56,59,70,71,75,82,89],"examples":[{"id":5,"stem":"A patient with OSA has a relatively collapsible pharynx and frequent arousals before upper-airway dilator recruitment. Which physiological trait most directly explains the premature awakening?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":7},{"id":10,"stem":"Which of the following statements about awake upper airway collapsibility index is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":9},{"id":11,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: choanal and epiglottic pressure catheters during negative-pressure inspiration; % collapsibility = (choanal − epiglottic)/choanal. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":9},{"id":18,"stem":"A patient has severe anatomic collapsibility despite adequate tongue-muscle recruitment. Which PALM component primarily describes the problem?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":11},{"id":21,"stem":"A patient awakens repeatedly before upper-airway dilator muscles can recruit adequately. Which endotype is most implicated?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":12},{"id":24,"stem":"An obese adult has recurrent obstructive events with ventilatory overshoot and undershoot despite moderate anatomy. Which non-anatomical mechanism is implicated?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":13},{"id":38,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: retrognathia, micrognathia, maxillary constriction with high-arched/narrow palate, steep mandibular plane angle, excessive vertical facial growth, inferiorly positioned hyoid, reduced maxillary/mandibular…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":18},{"id":40,"stem":"Which of the following statements about craniofacial is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":19},{"id":42,"stem":"A patient with severe pharyngeal collapsibility and high loop gain does not normalise on maximal comfortable mandibular advancement. Why might further protrusion fail?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":19},{"id":56,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP withdrawn for 5 breaths, repeated 5–10×. Peak inspiratory flow as % of CPAP-supported baseline; PIF ≥30% = only mildly collapsible airway; higher PIF = more negative Pcrit. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":25},{"id":59,"stem":"Which of the following statements about CPAP dial-down is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":26},{"id":70,"stem":"Which of the following statements about obesity is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":31},{"id":71,"stem":"Which of the following statements about non-anatomical traits is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":32},{"id":75,"stem":"Which of the following statements about obesity is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":33},{"id":82,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: macroglossia, elongated soft palate, enlarged uvula, adenotonsillar hypertrophy (dominant in children), parapharyngeal fat pads, large mandibular tori, lateral pharyngeal wall thickening. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":36},{"id":89,"stem":"Which of the following statements about soft tissue is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":39}],"count":16,"pdf_page":13},{"id":"1.4","title":"Prevalence and comorbidities","text":"OSA is associated with hypertension, cardiovascular and metabolic disease, but the magnitude of association and effect of treatment depend on the studied population. Do not imply that an appliance has proved prevention of a specific event for an individual.","question_ids":[13,14,41,43,58,61,72,77],"examples":[{"id":13,"stem":"Which of the following statements about gender is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":10},{"id":14,"stem":"Which of the following statements about gender is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":10},{"id":41,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: obesity, hypertension, cardiovascular disease (especially ischaemic heart disease), type 2 diabetes. Long-term: neurocognitive impairment, metabolic syndrome, systemic hypertension, cardiovascular disease. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":19},{"id":43,"stem":"Which of the following statements about predictive comorbidities is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":20},{"id":58,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI>5 — 9% women / 24% men; AHI>5 with sleepiness — 2% / 4%; AHI>15 — 4% / 9% (Wisconsin 1993). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":26},{"id":61,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about Prevalence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":27},{"id":72,"stem":"Which of the following statements about additional predisposing factors is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":32},{"id":77,"stem":"Which of the following statements about additional predisposing factors is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":34}],"count":8,"pdf_page":15},{"id":"1.5","title":"Age, sex and other risk factors","text":"Obesity, age, sex, craniofacial restriction and medications modify pre-test probability; patients without obesity and women presenting with fatigue or insomnia can still have clinically important OSA.","question_ids":[16,17,30,44,45,60,63,74,79],"examples":[{"id":16,"stem":"Which of the following statements about women more often present atypically is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":11},{"id":17,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: insomnia, fatigue, mood disturbance rather than classic witnessed apnoea — and are therefore under-diagnosed. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":11},{"id":30,"stem":"A post-menopausal woman reports fatigue and insomnia rather than loud snoring. Which approach best avoids under-recognition of OSA?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":15},{"id":44,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: roughly a 2:1 to 3:1 ratio at any given threshold. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":20},{"id":45,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about SDB is substantially more prevalent in men is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":20},{"id":60,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: adenotonsillar hypertrophy predominates in younger children (peak 2–8 years, when tonsillar tissue is proportionally largest), while overweight becomes the major factor in ad- olescence. Gender. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":27},{"id":63,"stem":"Which of the following statements about in children the dominant risk factor shifts with age is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":28},{"id":74,"stem":"Which of the following statements about smoking is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":33},{"id":79,"stem":"Which of the following statements about smoking is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":34}],"count":9,"pdf_page":16},{"id":"1.6","title":"Signs and symptoms","text":"Witnessed apnoeas, choking, habitual snoring and sleepiness raise suspicion. No combination of symptoms, ESS or STOP-BANG alone confirms OSA; use a medical diagnostic pathway.","question_ids":[1,2,6],"examples":[{"id":1,"stem":"An adult describes loud snoring, witnessed pauses, gasping awakenings and morning headache. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":6},{"id":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old taxi driver reports loud nightly snoring, witnessed breathing pauses and episodes of nearly falling asleep while driving. He asks for a snoring splint. What is the most appropriate immediate response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":6},{"id":6,"stem":"A patient denies sleepiness yet a partner witnesses recurrent apnoeas. What is the best conclusion?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":7}],"count":3,"pdf_page":17},{"id":"1.7","title":"Impact on the individual","text":"Assess safety-critical consequences first: unintentional dozing while driving, near misses, occupational risk, and severe daytime impairment warrant prompt medical assessment and appropriate safety advice.","question_ids":[19,20,39],"examples":[{"id":19,"stem":"Which of the following statements about public safety is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":12},{"id":20,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: impaired reflexes and judgement → higher risk of serious and fatal crashes; workplace accidents. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":12},{"id":39,"stem":"A professional driver with suspected OSA reports dozing at traffic lights. What is the most important immediate concern?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":18}],"count":3,"pdf_page":17},{"id":"1.8","title":"Impact on society","text":"The societal consequences of untreated sleep disorders include reduced alertness, transport and workplace accidents, productivity loss and bed-partner disturbance; the patient’s occupation changes urgency.","question_ids":[9,12],"examples":[{"id":9,"stem":"A bus driver with untreated sleepiness asks why the condition matters outside personal health. Which societal consequence is most immediate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":8},{"id":12,"stem":"An employer reports absenteeism and reduced work performance in staff with untreated sleep disorders. Which domain is being illustrated?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":9}],"count":2,"pdf_page":18},{"id":"1.9","title":"Insomnia","text":"Chronic insomnia requires persistent sleep difficulty with adequate opportunity and daytime impairment, usually at least three nights weekly for three months. CBT-I is first-line; persistent insomnia needs its own assessment alongside OSA.","question_ids":[4,22,23,27,46,47,62,65,84,91,92,99,102,106,109,113],"examples":[{"id":4,"stem":"A 44-year-old reports difficulty initiating sleep four nights per week for five months, with daytime impairment despite adequate opportunity to sleep. Which diagnosis is best supported?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":7},{"id":22,"stem":"Which of the following statements about short-term insomnia disorder is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":13},{"id":23,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: clinically significant dissatisfaction or impairment failing those frequency/duration criteria. Other insomnia disorder — rare cases not meeting short-term criteria but warranting attention. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":13},{"id":27,"stem":"An adult complains of difficulty sleeping four nights per week for four months with impaired daytime function. How should the duration criterion be classified?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":14},{"id":46,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: sleep-onset and/or sleep-maintenance complaints with associated daytime impairment, exceeding minimal frequency and duration thresholds shown to be associated with clinically significant morbidity. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":21},{"id":47,"stem":"Which of the following statements about chronic insomnia disorder is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":21},{"id":62,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: 33–50% of the adult population; about one third report dissatisfaction with sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":28},{"id":65,"stem":"Which of the following statements about insomnia symptoms is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":29},{"id":84,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: marked, durable improvement in SOL, WASO and sleep efficiency; only a small improvement in total sleep time, augmenting over time. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":37},{"id":91,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about effect on outcomes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":39},{"id":92,"stem":"Which of the following statements about treatment is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":40},{"id":99,"stem":"Which of the following statements about treatment is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":42},{"id":102,"stem":"Which of the following statements about risk factors is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":43},{"id":106,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: symptoms 33–50%; ~⅓ report dissatisfaction with sleep; with distress/impairment 10–15%; chronic 5–15%; with medical/psychiatric comorbidity 50–75%. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":44},{"id":109,"stem":"Which of the following statements about risk factors is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":45},{"id":113,"stem":"Which of the following statements about Prevalence is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":47}],"count":16,"pdf_page":18},{"id":"1.10","title":"Other respiratory sleep disorders","text":"Central apnoea lacks respiratory effort, whereas obstruction has continued effort. Suspected central apnoea, hypoventilation or complex respiratory disease changes the appropriate test and treatment; do not treat these as straightforward OSA.","question_ids":[25,49,67,81,86,93,94,100,101,107,108,112,115,116,119,120,122,123,124,126,127,128,129,130],"examples":[{"id":25,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about complementary is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":14},{"id":49,"stem":"Which of the following statements about sleep-related hypoxaemia disorder is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":22},{"id":67,"stem":"Which of the following statements about aetiologies is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":30},{"id":81,"stem":"Which of the following statements about CPAP in those already committed or willing is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":35},{"id":86,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: heavy snoring (>50% of sleep time) may induce endothelial injury at the carotid bifurcation given its proximity to the hypopharyngeal walls → increased intima-media thickness, carotid atherosclerosis, metabolic syndrome. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":38},{"id":93,"stem":"Which of the following statements about not benign is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":40},{"id":94,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: septoplasty with turbinate reduction only where there are both symptoms and objective nasendoscopic evidence of obstruction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":40},{"id":100,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: pre-phase + adjunctive, adjunctive + mainstream, or multiple mainstream. Higher efficacy, limited evidence. 1.11 Other non-respiratory sleep disorders. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":42},{"id":101,"stem":"Which of the following statements about nasal surgery is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":43},{"id":107,"stem":"Which of the following statements about combination therapy is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":45},{"id":108,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: slow, monotone, groaning expiratory vocalisation; benign, often mistaken for a parasomnia. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":45},{"id":112,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: hypercapnic CSA, reflecting either abnormal central pattern generator output (\"won't breathe\") or impairment of respiratory motor output caudal to the pattern generator (\"can't breathe\"). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":47},{"id":115,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about catathrenia is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":48},{"id":116,"stem":"Which of the following statements about complications is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":48},{"id":119,"stem":"Which of the following statements about classification by awake co2 is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":49},{"id":120,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: insufficient alveolar ventilation causing CO2 retention and hypoxaemia. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":50},{"id":122,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: one-way resistor valves creating positive end-expiratory pressure to stabilise the airway; overall improvement but less than CPAP; minimally harmful, costly long term. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":50},{"id":123,"stem":"Which of the following statements about complications is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":51},{"id":124,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: physical barrier or a position-sensing vibratory device. Reduces snoring frequency and rate in most non-apnoeic snorers (AHI<5) but not in those with OSA; older devices had poor long-term adherence. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":51},{"id":126,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: approximately one third of patients with OSA do not snore, and about one third of snorers have no OSA (AHI <5). Snoring therefore neither rules OSA in nor out — the single most useful sentence on this subject. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":52},{"id":127,"stem":"Which of the following statements about hypoventilation syndromes is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":52},{"id":128,"stem":"Which of the following statements about nasal EPAP is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":52},{"id":129,"stem":"Which of the following statements about positional therapy in supine-dominant snorers is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":53},{"id":130,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about relationship to OSA is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":53}],"count":24,"pdf_page":20},{"id":"1.11","title":"Other non-respiratory sleep disorders","text":"Distinguish NREM parasomnias, REM behaviour disorder, narcolepsy and restless legs/periodic limb movement problems by timing, dream enactment, cataplexy and movement history. They may coexist with OSA.","question_ids":[26,28,33,48,51,64,69],"examples":[{"id":26,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: Delayed phase (adolescents), advanced phase (older adults), irregular and non-24-hour rhythms, shift work disorder, jet lag. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":14},{"id":28,"stem":"Which of the following statements about circadian rhythm sleep–wake disorders is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":15},{"id":33,"stem":"A sleepy patient suddenly loses muscle tone when laughing and enters REM unusually quickly. Which disorder must be considered by a sleep physician?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":16},{"id":48,"stem":"An adult with irregular shifts reports fatigue and reduced daytime concentration. The clinical team records the following finding: Narcolepsy type 1 — hypocretin/orexin deficiency with cataplexy; type 2 without. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":22},{"id":51,"stem":"Which of the following statements about central disorders of hypersomnolence is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":23},{"id":64,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: Restless legs syndrome (Willis–Ekbom) — urge to move with creeping/crawling sensations, worse in the evening and at rest, relieved by movement; associated with iron deficiency and dopaminergic dysfunction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":29},{"id":69,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about sleep-related movement disorders is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":31}],"count":7,"pdf_page":23},{"id":"1.12","title":"Medications and sleep","text":"Alcohol, opioids, sedatives, antidepressants and stimulants can alter architecture, respiratory control or bruxism. Identify the agent and its mechanism; do not assume all sedatives have identical effects in all phenotypes.","question_ids":[29,31,36,50,53,66,76,83,88,95,96,103,104,110,111,114,117,118,121,125],"examples":[{"id":29,"stem":"Which of the following statements about alcohol is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":15},{"id":31,"stem":"Which of the following statements about alcohol is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":16},{"id":36,"stem":"A patient with suspected OSA takes an opioid at bedtime and develops more complex events. What is the key concern?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":17},{"id":50,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: an adenosine receptor antagonist (blocking the mediator of homeostatic sleep pressure) with a half-life of roughly five hours. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":23},{"id":53,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about caffeine is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":24},{"id":66,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: ~70% have ≥1; loop gain ~36%, low arousal threshold ~37%, poor muscle responsiveness ~36%; Pcrit distribution ~19% / 58% / 23%. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":30},{"id":76,"stem":"A 51-year-old with witnessed respiratory pauses has a consultation with a sleep specialist. The clinical team records the following finding: symptoms 33–50% · disorder 10–15% · chronic 5–15% · comorbid 50–75% · chronic = ≥3 nights/week × ≥3 months. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":33},{"id":83,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about insomnia is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":36},{"id":88,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about OSA–TMD is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":38},{"id":95,"stem":"Which of the following statements about OSA–TMD is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":41},{"id":96,"stem":"Which of the following statements about opioids is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":41},{"id":103,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. Which of the following statements about opioids is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":43},{"id":104,"stem":"Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":44},{"id":110,"stem":"A patient referred from a dental clinic reports fragmented sleep and morning fatigue. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI>5 — 9% F / 24% M (Wisconsin 1993); moderate–severe — 6% F / 13% M (2007–2010); global 936 million / 425 million moderate–severe. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":46},{"id":111,"stem":"Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":46},{"id":114,"stem":"A 63-year-old with recurrent nocturnal awakenings is being assessed for disordered breathing. The clinical team records the following finding: stimulant plus nocturnal withdrawal arousals; smoking also causes upper airway inflammation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":48},{"id":117,"stem":"Which of the following statements about OSA prevalence is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":49},{"id":118,"stem":"A 48-year-old with snoring and unrefreshing sleep asks about the underlying sleep physiology. The clinical team records the following finding: ≥2 OSA signs/symptoms → 73% greater incidence of first-onset TMD; chronic TMD >3× more frequent in high-OSA-likelihood adults. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":49},{"id":121,"stem":"Which of the following statements about nicotine is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":50},{"id":125,"stem":"Which of the following statements about OSA and TMD is most accurate in dental sleep medicine?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":51}],"count":20,"pdf_page":24}],"cases":[{"situation":"A thin patient with retrognathia has witnessed apnoeas but a normal BMI.","decision":"Refer for a diagnostic sleep evaluation; craniofacial risk matters even without obesity.","why":"BMI alone cannot rule out obstruction, and screening tools are not diagnostic."},{"situation":"A patient with OSA has persistent events despite progressively greater protrusion, and a high-loop-gain phenotype is suspected.","decision":"Request multidisciplinary reassessment and consider alternative or combination therapy rather than unlimited advancement.","why":"The appliance does not reliably correct unstable ventilatory control."},{"situation":"A sleepy commercial driver reports dozing at traffic lights.","decision":"Prioritise urgent medical evaluation and immediate discussion of driving safety under local rules.","why":"Routine annual review is not an adequate response to an active safety risk."}],"pitfalls":["Percentages describing non-anatomical endotypes or Pcrit distributions come from particular cohorts; they are not a patient-level diagnostic rule.","Association does not establish causation: bruxism, TMD and OSA can overlap without one invariably causing another."],"cue":"For an SBA asking why further protrusion is failing, consider whether the limitation is dental tolerance, adherence, anatomy or a non-anatomical endotype.","pages":{"id":1,"key":[7,8],"quick":[7,8],"detail":[9,27]},"count":130},{"id":2,"title":"Diagnostic sleep testing and interpretation","short":"Diagnostic sleep testing and interpretation","lens":"Choose the appropriate test, interpret denominators and distinguish clinically significant residual disease from symptomatic improvement.","sections":[{"id":"2.1","title":"Normal PSG architecture and parameters","text":"Polysomnography (PSG) records sleep staging as well as respiratory and physiological channels. Total sleep time allows an AHI per hour asleep, while many HSAT systems use monitoring time for an REI.","question_ids":[155,156,171,172,185,186,199,200,209,210,215,216,223,224,231,232,237,238],"examples":[{"id":155,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about eeg + eog + emg together determine sleep stage is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":62},{"id":156,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: No staging is possible without them — the fundamental limitation of level 3 and 4 studies. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":62},{"id":171,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about effort is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":67},{"id":172,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about effort is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":67},{"id":185,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about end-tidal or transcutaneous co2 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":73},{"id":186,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: hypoventilation; a standard measurement in paediatric PSG. Video/audio — parasomnia, seizure, positional confirmation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":73},{"id":199,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about oxygen saturation nadir is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":77},{"id":200,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: The minimum oxygen level contributes information on hypoxaemic severity. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":78},{"id":209,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about body position is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":81},{"id":210,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: positional dependence; directly relevant to appliance and positional therapy decisions. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":82},{"id":215,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about airflow is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":84},{"id":216,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about airflow is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":84},{"id":223,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about eeg (scalp) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":87},{"id":224,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: sleep staging and arousals. EOG (periorbital) — eye movements, identifies REM and sleep onset. EMG (submental, tibial) — muscle tone/REM atonia, leg movements; masseter EMG for bruxism. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":88},{"id":231,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about RIP mechanism is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":90},{"id":232,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about RIP mechanism is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":91},{"id":237,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about NREM 75–80% dominating the first half, REM 20–25% dominating the second is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":92},{"id":238,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: Arousal index low, scored by the ASDA 3-second rule. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":93}],"count":18,"pdf_page":30},{"id":"2.2","title":"Abnormal respiratory and sleep indices","text":"Adult AHI severity bands are conventionally 5–<15, 15–<30 and >=30 events/hour. Consider event definitions, oxygen burden, sleep stage, position, symptoms and comorbidity rather than treating one cutoff as the whole diagnosis.","question_ids":[131,132,133,136,137,138,139,158,159,173,174,187,188,201,202,217,218,225,226,233,234,239,240,243,244],"examples":[{"id":131,"stem":"A respiratory event shows a 95% airflow drop lasting 14 seconds while thoracoabdominal effort continues. Which classification is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":54},{"id":132,"stem":"During polysomnography, airflow falls by 95% for 13 seconds while thoracic and abdominal respiratory effort continues. How should this respiratory event be classified?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":54},{"id":133,"stem":"A study records a 40% airflow reduction for 15 seconds accompanied by an EEG arousal but no 4% oxygen desaturation. How should the event be approached under the recommended AASM hypopnoea rule?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":54},{"id":136,"stem":"A 12-second airflow reduction of 40% is associated with a cortical arousal but no 4% oxygen fall. How is it scored under the recommended AASM rule?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":55},{"id":137,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about chronic insomnia on PSG is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":56},{"id":138,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: increased sleep latency and/or WASO (often >30 min) with reduced sleep efficiency for age. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":56},{"id":139,"stem":"An adult has a PSG AHI of 22 events per hour. Which severity category applies?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":56},{"id":158,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about respiratory effort-related arousal (RERA) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":63},{"id":159,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: Arousal following increasing effort or flattened airflow that does not meet apnoea or hypopnoea criteria. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":63},{"id":173,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about fragmentation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":68},{"id":174,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: increased arousal index, increased stage shifts, increased N1, reduced slow-wave and REM sleep: the signature of untreated OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":68},{"id":187,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about respiratory disturbance index is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":73},{"id":188,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI plus respiratory effort-related arousals per hour of sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":74},{"id":201,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about apnoea-hypopnoea index is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":78},{"id":202,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: Total scored apnoeas and hypopnoeas divided by hours of sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":78},{"id":217,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about severity is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":85},{"id":218,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: adult: normal <5 · mild 5–<15 · moderate 15–<30 · severe ≥30. Paediatric: <1 · 1–<5 · 5–<10 · ≥10. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":85},{"id":225,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about apnoea is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":88},{"id":226,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about apnoea is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":88},{"id":233,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about irregular or absent stages is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":91},{"id":234,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: irregular cycling or missing stages point to specific disorders — for example, a patient with narcolepsy may enter sleep directly into REM (sleep-onset REM period). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":91},{"id":239,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about hypopnoea is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":93},{"id":240,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: reduction in airflow and/or thoracoabdominal movement of ≥30% of baseline for ≥10 s, with ≥3% desaturation and/or an arousal. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":94},{"id":243,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about irregular or absent stages point to specific disorders is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":95},{"id":244,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: narcolepsy patients may enter sleep directly into REM. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":95}],"count":25,"pdf_page":33},{"id":"2.3","title":"PSG interpretation and treatment choices","text":"The AASM-recommended hypopnoea rule includes >=30% flow reduction for >=10 seconds with >=3% desaturation or arousal; the alternative >=4% rule can yield a different index. Identify which scoring rule was used before comparing studies.","question_ids":[140,141,160,161,162,175,176,189,190],"examples":[{"id":140,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about surgery is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":57},{"id":141,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about surgery is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":57},{"id":160,"stem":"A dentist reviews a study revealing substantial central apnoeas. What is the next step?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":63},{"id":161,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about oral appliance therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":64},{"id":162,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: for mild to moderate OSA, or severe OSA where CPAP is refused, not tolerated or ineffective. Positional dependence increases the likelihood of success. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":64},{"id":175,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about pharmacotherapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":68},{"id":176,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: used for other disorders diagnosed on the study (e.g. RBD) or for residual sleepiness; it is not a primary treatment for OSA itself. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":69},{"id":189,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about behavioural therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":74},{"id":190,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. The clinical team records the following finding: positional therapy where the study shows supine dependence; weight loss where obesity is present. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":74}],"count":9,"pdf_page":34},{"id":"2.4","title":"Assessment of therapeutic response","text":"A decrease in AHI does not itself establish satisfactory residual disease control, and an improved ESS does not exclude persistent OSA. The medical sleep provider confirms efficacy with objective follow-up testing.","question_ids":[143,144,154,163,164,177,178,191,192],"examples":[{"id":143,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about treatment failure is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":58},{"id":144,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. The clinical team records the following finding: ongoing clinical symptoms, with or without <50% AHI reduction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":58},{"id":154,"stem":"Baseline AHI is 32; with the appliance it is 8 and symptoms improve. Which label is most appropriate in the notebook?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":61},{"id":163,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about partial response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":64},{"id":164,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. The clinical team records the following finding: improved symptoms and ≥50% AHI reduction, but AHI remains ≥5/h. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":65},{"id":177,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about signature of a good outcome is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":69},{"id":178,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: Sleep: REM increases, NREM decreases; TST typically unchanged — significant redistribution to more REM (REM rebound), not more sleep; arousal index decreases. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":70},{"id":191,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about complete response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":75},{"id":192,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: resolution of OSA symptoms and treatment AHI <5/h. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":75}],"count":9,"pdf_page":35},{"id":"2.5","title":"Attended vs unattended studies","text":"An uncomplicated adult with high pre-test probability may be appropriate for HSAT under a clinical pathway. A negative, inconclusive or technically inadequate HSAT despite suspicion calls for PSG; significant cardiopulmonary disease, chronic opioids, stroke, severe insomnia or suspected hypoventilation favor PSG.","question_ids":[135,142,145,146,147,165,166,179,180,193,194,203,204,211,212,219,220,227,228],"examples":[{"id":135,"stem":"A patient with suspected OSA also has significant heart failure and possible central apnoeas. Which diagnostic investigation is generally most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":55},{"id":142,"stem":"A patient has significant heart failure, possible central apnoea and severe daytime sleepiness. Which diagnostic test is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":57},{"id":145,"stem":"A type 3 home sleep study reports REI 12 with long wake periods. What limitation should the dentist explain?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":58},{"id":146,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about type 1 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":59},{"id":147,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about type 1 is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":59},{"id":165,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about type 3 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":65},{"id":166,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about type 3 is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":65},{"id":179,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about type 2 indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":70},{"id":180,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: high probability of moderate–severe OSA without significant medical comorbidity or other suspected sleep disorder. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":71},{"id":193,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about type 2 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":75},{"id":194,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about type 2 is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":76},{"id":203,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about type 4 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":79},{"id":204,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about type 4 is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":79},{"id":211,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about high-risk patient with negative hsat is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":82},{"id":212,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: The physician should consider attended polysomnography when clinical suspicion remains high. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":83},{"id":219,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about type 1 indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":85},{"id":220,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: low probability of moderate–severe OSA; unsuitable for a type 2 study; failed type 2 study. Also the standard for narcolepsy, central sleep apnoea, parasomnias and movement disorders. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":86},{"id":227,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about type 1 advantages is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":89},{"id":228,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about type 1 advantages is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":89}],"count":19,"pdf_page":36},{"id":"2.6","title":"Vigilance testing: MSLT and MWT","text":"MSLT evaluates pathological sleep propensity and sleep-onset REM periods; MWT evaluates wake-maintenance ability. These tests answer different questions and do not substitute for an OSA diagnostic study.","question_ids":[134,148,149,150,167,168,181,182,195,196,205,206],"examples":[{"id":134,"stem":"A patient with suspected narcolepsy has excessive sleepiness despite adequate sleep and an otherwise unremarkable respiratory assessment. Which test best evaluates sleep propensity and sleep-onset REM periods?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":55},{"id":148,"stem":"A patient is being investigated for narcolepsy after an overnight PSG. Which next-day test quantifies propensity to fall asleep and SOREMPs?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":59},{"id":149,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about MWT interpretation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":60},{"id":150,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: a longer mean sleep latency indicates greater ability to maintain wakefulness; considered a more accurate reflection of the ability to function and maintain alertness in common situations of inactivity. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":60},{"id":167,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about MSLT indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":66},{"id":168,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: diagnosing and quantifying physiological sleepiness in narcolepsy and idiopathic hypersomnia. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":66},{"id":181,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about MSLT method is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":71},{"id":182,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. The clinical team records the following finding: follows an overnight PSG to confirm adequate prior sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":72},{"id":195,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about MWT method is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":76},{"id":196,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: the patient sits up in a chair in a quiet dimly lit room, instructed to stay awake; no vocalisation or movement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":76},{"id":205,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about MSLT interpretation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":79},{"id":206,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: mean sleep latency averaged across naps — shorter = more severe sleepiness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":80}],"count":12,"pdf_page":37},{"id":"2.7","title":"Screening and dental findings","text":"ESS measures sleep propensity; STOP-BANG and related tools estimate risk. They direct referral or testing but neither grade disease severity reliably nor provide a definitive diagnosis.","question_ids":[151,152,153,157,169,170,183,184,197,198,207,208,213,214,221,222,229,230,235,236,241,242,245],"examples":[{"id":151,"stem":"A STOP-BANG score of five is recorded at dental assessment. How should it be used?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":60},{"id":152,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about macroglossia is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":61},{"id":153,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about macroglossia is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":61},{"id":157,"stem":"A patient has a high ESS but a low respiratory event index. What is the best interpretation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":62},{"id":169,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about modified mallampati is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":66},{"id":170,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: describes oral airway patency. Technique: patient sits upright, opens widely, protrudes the tongue maximally without phonation (the \"without phonation\" distinguishes the modified from the original anaesthetic…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":67},{"id":183,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about sensors is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":72},{"id":184,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about sensors is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":72},{"id":197,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about australian direct-referral pathway is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":77},{"id":198,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: positive questionnaire (Berlin positive / OSA50 ≥5 / STOP-BANG ≥4) plus ESS ≥8. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":77},{"id":207,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. Which of the following statements about mouth breathing with dry mouth is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":80},{"id":208,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. The clinical team records the following finding: compensation for nasal obstruction. Bruxism — common in disrupted sleep and airway problems. Dental erosion — may indicate GORD, which frequently coexists. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":81},{"id":213,"stem":"A 57-year-old with witnessed apnoeas undergoes diagnostic polysomnography. Which of the following statements about australian direct referral is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":83},{"id":214,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. The clinical team records the following finding: positive questionnaire (Berlin / OSA50 ≥5 / STOP-BANG ≥4) plus ESS ≥8. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":84},{"id":221,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about screening thresholds is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":86},{"id":222,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: ESS >10 · STOP-BANG ≥3 · OSA50 ≥5 · Berlin ≥2 categories · NoSAS ≥8 · PSQ >0.33. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":87},{"id":229,"stem":"A sleep physician is reviewing a home sleep apnoea study with a patient who snores. Which of the following statements about high palatal vault / narrow arch is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":89},{"id":230,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: maxillary constriction reduces oral cavity space; narrow tapered V-shaped maxillary arch, possibly with posterior crossbites or compensatory mandibular constriction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":90},{"id":235,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. Which of the following statements about OSA diagnosis is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":92},{"id":236,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. The clinical team records the following finding: RDI >15/h, or >5/h with symptoms. AHI ≥15 → material increase in metabolic and vascular risk. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":92},{"id":241,"stem":"A patient with cardiopulmonary comorbidity is being considered for objective sleep testing. Which of the following statements about epworth sleepiness scale threshold is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":94},{"id":242,"stem":"A patient on treatment asks whether a favourable symptom report proves normal breathing during sleep. The clinical team records the following finding: More than ten points of the possible twenty-four suggests excessive daytime sleepiness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":95},{"id":245,"stem":"A 60-year-old with suspected OSA asks how a sleep-study result should be interpreted. The clinical team records the following finding: Snoring, Tiredness, Observed apnoea, Pressure (hypertension), BMI >35, Age >50, Neck >40 cm, Gender male. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":96}],"count":23,"pdf_page":38}],"cases":[{"situation":"An adult with witnessed apnoeas has a technically inadequate home study.","decision":"Arrange the diagnostic pathway for attended PSG, through the medical sleep provider.","why":"A nondiagnostic home result must not be interpreted as a negative OSA diagnosis."},{"situation":"An oral appliance user reports no snoring, but treatment testing shows substantial residual AHI.","decision":"Inform the medical provider and reconsider calibration or alternative/combination treatment.","why":"Subjective improvement is not objective efficacy."},{"situation":"A home report lists REI 8/h, while a later PSG shows AHI 18/h.","decision":"Examine recording-time versus sleep-time denominators, REM/position sampling and scoring rules.","why":"The difference is not necessarily a true rapid deterioration in disease."}],"pitfalls":["AHI, RDI and REI are not interchangeable labels; check precisely which events and denominator a laboratory used.","Do not prescribe a device solely because a STOP-BANG or ESS threshold is reached."],"cue":"Before interpreting an index, ask: “What events were counted, over what denominator, using which scoring criterion?”","pages":{"id":2,"key":[28,29],"quick":[28,29],"detail":[30,46]},"count":115},{"id":3,"title":"Oral appliance therapy and other treatments","short":"Oral appliances and alternative treatment","lens":"Select the appropriate treatment based on diagnosis, patient preference, disease severity, tolerability and objective verification.","sections":[{"id":"3.1","title":"History of OAT","text":"Oral appliance therapy (OAT) has evolved from simple protrusive devices to custom, titratable appliances with documented calibration. Historical designs matter mainly when explaining why device fit and adjustability affect outcomes.","question_ids":[274,279,296,299,316,319,334,337,344,347,354,357,360,363,364,367],"examples":[{"id":274,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: first suggested breathing affects sleep quality: nasal rather than mouth breathing gives more restful, better quality sleep, better daytime function and better general health. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":106},{"id":279,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about george catlin (19th c.) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":108},{"id":296,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: the monobloc, for glossoptosis. ~30 years later used to reposition the mandible. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":115},{"id":299,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about pierre robin, 1903 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":116},{"id":316,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: the tongue retaining device, holding the tongue forward by suction; historically the second most common type. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":124},{"id":319,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about cartwright & samelson, 1982 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":125},{"id":334,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: made from digital or physical impressions/models; not prefabricated; biocompatible material; engages both arches. Non-custom (\"boil-and-bite\") — prefabricated, only partially modified. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":131},{"id":337,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about custom oa is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":132},{"id":344,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: allows varying protrusion (the analogue of CPAP titration). Non-titratable — single position, no change possible. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":136},{"id":347,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about titratable is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":137},{"id":354,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: a device intended to protrude and stabilise the mandible to maintain a patent airway during sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":140},{"id":357,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about oral appliance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":142},{"id":360,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: focus on mandibular advancement appliances; case reports gave way to RCTs confirming efficacy in mild– moderate and more recently severe OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":143},{"id":363,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about modern era is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":144},{"id":364,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: advancement in increments of 1 mm or less; protrusive adjustment range ≥5 mm. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":145},{"id":367,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about effective oral appliance (aadsm 2019) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":146}],"count":16,"pdf_page":49},{"id":"3.2","title":"Mechanism of OAT","text":"Mandibular advancement generally enlarges or stabilises the upper airway by advancing the jaw and associated soft tissues; the anatomical response is variable and cannot be inferred from a single cephalometric measurement.","question_ids":[246,251],"examples":[{"id":246,"stem":"A patient asks why a protrusive device can reduce retropalatal obstruction although it does not directly contact the soft palate. What is the best explanation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":97},{"id":251,"stem":"Why can OAT fail in a patient with severe upper-airway collapsibility and high loop gain despite excellent nightly wear?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":98}],"count":2,"pdf_page":50},{"id":"3.3","title":"Indications and contraindications","text":"Custom, titratable OAT is a supported option for patients who prefer it or cannot tolerate PAP, following medical diagnosis. Assess retention, periodontal stability, protrusive range, TMD symptoms and expectations before choosing a device.","question_ids":[247,252,254,277,281,298,301,318,321],"examples":[{"id":247,"stem":"A 48-year-old with physician-diagnosed mild OSA prefers mandibular advancement therapy. There is sufficient dental support and no active TMJ pain. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":97},{"id":252,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about advantages, including over CPAP is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":99},{"id":254,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed moderate OSA cannot tolerate CPAP and has stable dentition. Which treatment is guideline-consistent?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":99},{"id":277,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about indications is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":107},{"id":281,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":109},{"id":298,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: OSA must first be excluded by a physician before oral appliance therapy for primary snoring. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":116},{"id":301,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about OSA and primary snoring distinction is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":117},{"id":318,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: Division of responsibility: diagnosis, prescription and monitoring of effectiveness rest with the medical practitioner. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":125},{"id":321,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about side effects are usually well tolerated once symptomatic relief is achieved is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":126}],"count":9,"pdf_page":51},{"id":"3.4","title":"PAP therapies","text":"PAP usually reduces respiratory-event frequency more than OAT when worn. Mask fit, pressure intolerance and adherence can alter real-world benefit; the medical team should troubleshoot PAP rather than dismiss it after one difficult night.","question_ids":[248,255,257,280,283,300,303,320,323,336,339,346,349,356,359,362,365,366,369],"examples":[{"id":248,"stem":"A patient with confirmed OSA on CPAP complains of nasal dryness and congestion. Which is a reasonable first-line supportive intervention?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":97},{"id":255,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about monitoring is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":100},{"id":257,"stem":"A patient has moderate-to-severe OSA and asks which treatment suppresses AHI most during use. Which is the strongest answer?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":100},{"id":280,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: treatment must be based on a diagnosis established by objective sleep apnoea testing. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":108},{"id":283,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about good practice is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":109},{"id":300,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: dry mouth and throat, nasal discharge and congestion, dry or bleeding nose, sinus pain, headache, sore throat, hoarse voice and reduced smell. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":117},{"id":303,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about heated humidification is suggested to reduce side effects is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":118},{"id":320,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: Around four centimetres of water pressure. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":126},{"id":323,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about initial adult pap titration pressure is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":127},{"id":336,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: establish the minimum pressure at which snoring ceases and apnoeic events are absent. Start 4 cmH2O, titrate to the lowest effective pressure, maximum ~20 cmH2O. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":132},{"id":339,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about titration is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":133},{"id":346,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: reduces dry mouth and throat, nasal discharge and congestion, dry or bleeding nose, sinus pain, headache, sore throat, hoarse voice, reduced smell. Trade-offs: \"rain-out\", cost, maintenance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":137},{"id":349,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about heated humidification suggested is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":138},{"id":356,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: recommend either CPAP or APAP for ongoing treatment (no difference in adherence, sleepiness or quality of life; APAP's advantage is automatic adjustment for alcohol, position and weight change). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":141},{"id":359,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about modality is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":142},{"id":362,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: lower compliance and tolerance, especially in mild OSA — 40–60% at 12 months, failure rates 29–83%. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":144},{"id":365,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about disadvantages is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":145},{"id":366,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about advantages is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":145},{"id":369,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about advantages is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":146}],"count":19,"pdf_page":52},{"id":"3.5","title":"Surgery","text":"Surgical options depend on anatomy, patient goals and objective disease characteristics. No operation guarantees cure; residual OSA and objective reassessment remain relevant.","question_ids":[253,258,266,282,285,302,305],"examples":[{"id":253,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: genioglossus advancement; midline glossectomy; lingual tonsillectomy; transoral robotic surgery; tongue coblation for retroglossal prolapse or hypertrophic lingual tonsil. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":99},{"id":258,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about tongue base is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":101},{"id":266,"stem":"Drug-induced sleep endoscopy identifies complete concentric palatal collapse in a patient considered for hypoglossal stimulation. What is the implication?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":103},{"id":282,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: More than fifty percent reduction in AHI with postoperative AHI below twenty. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":109},{"id":285,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about uppp success convention is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":110},{"id":302,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: septoplasty; inferior turbinectomy or turbinoplasty; nasal valve surgery for lateral wall collapse; endoscopic sinus surgery; polypectomy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":118},{"id":305,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about nasal (usually adjunctive/pre-phase) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":119}],"count":7,"pdf_page":55},{"id":"3.6","title":"Positional and behavioural therapies","text":"Weight management, positional therapy, alcohol reduction and sleep-hygiene measures may support a treatment plan, but behavioural advice alone may be insufficient for clinically significant OSA.","question_ids":[249,256,261,263,272,284,287,304,307,322,325],"examples":[{"id":249,"stem":"A patient has predominantly supine OSA with substantially fewer events when sleeping laterally. Which adjunctive strategy is most directly relevant?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":98},{"id":256,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: positional data from the PSG establish the indication. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":100},{"id":261,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about indication is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":102},{"id":263,"stem":"A patient with positional OSA has events almost exclusively when supine. What adjunct is most anatomically relevant?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":102},{"id":272,"stem":"An obese adult asks whether losing 10% of body weight is expected to cure OSA. What is the best counselling answer?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":105},{"id":284,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: Sleep behavioural therapy = sleep hygiene, sleeping position, weight control, avoidance of sedatives including alcohol. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":110},{"id":287,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about sleep hygiene and behavioural therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":111},{"id":304,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: promotes side sleeping instead of supine sleeping. Snoring severity and AHI are greater supine; in children the lowest AHI is often prone. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":119},{"id":307,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about principle is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":120},{"id":322,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about methods is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":126},{"id":325,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about methods is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":127}],"count":11,"pdf_page":58},{"id":"3.7","title":"Emerging therapies","text":"Newer pharmacologic or stimulation treatments have selected indications, constraints and changing evidence. Avoid presenting endotype-targeted investigational approaches as routine dental prescribing.","question_ids":[259,264,286,289,306,309,324,327,338,341,348,351],"examples":[{"id":259,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: Electrical stimulation of the hypoglossal nerve recruits tongue dilator function. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":101},{"id":264,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about hypoglossal stimulation anatomy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":103},{"id":286,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: recommended for residual excessive sleepiness in patients who remain sleepy despite effective PAP and lack any other identifiable cause. Not a treatment for the OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":111},{"id":289,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about modafinil / armodafinil is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":112},{"id":306,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: carbonic anhydrase inhibitor stabilising ventilatory control in the high loop gain endotype. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":120},{"id":309,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about acetazolamide is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":121},{"id":324,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: reduce both AHI and body weight in OSA with obesity, addressing metabolic and respiratory components together; the most significant recent pharmacological development. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":127},{"id":327,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about glp-1 receptor agonists (e.g. tirzepatide) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":128},{"id":338,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: targeted exercises strengthening upper airway muscles; reduces snoring and AHI in mild– moderate OSA or as an adjunct. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":133},{"id":341,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about orofacial myofunctional therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":134},{"id":348,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: target poor muscle responsiveness; significant AHI reduction and improved sleep quality, particularly in combination; long-term safety and selection under investigation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":138},{"id":351,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about noradrenergic–antimuscarinic combinations (atomoxetine + oxybutynin) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":139}],"count":12,"pdf_page":59},{"id":"3.8","title":"Combination therapies","text":"Combination therapy can include OAT with PAP or positional treatment in appropriately selected patients. It may improve comfort or residual control, but should be coordinated with the medical provider.","question_ids":[262,267,269,288,291,308,311,326,329,340,343,350,353],"examples":[{"id":262,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: for severe OSA, reduces AHI, ODI and required CPAP pressure versus either alone, and can improve PAP adherence by permitting a lower, more comfortable pressure. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":102},{"id":267,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about oat + CPAP (connected or not) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":104},{"id":269,"stem":"A patient requires high CPAP pressure and reports mask intolerance but can tolerate a mandibular appliance. What combined strategy may reduce pressure needs?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":104},{"id":288,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: significantly reduces AHI in incomplete responders to OAT alone; larger AHI reduction than MAS alone, though sleep efficiency is lower with the combination. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":112},{"id":291,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about oat + nasal or oral EPAP is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":113},{"id":308,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. The clinical team records the following finding: improves overall effectiveness and adherence by increasing time on treatment while reducing the burden of CPAP use and of appliance side effects. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":121},{"id":311,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about oat and CPAP used interchangeably is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":122},{"id":326,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: incremental reduction in oxybutynin; acetazolamide plus eszopiclone. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":128},{"id":329,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about CPAP + weight loss is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":129},{"id":340,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: for overall treatment effectiveness and adherence by severe OSA, reduces AHI, ODI and the required increasing the percentage of time on treatment, while. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":134},{"id":343,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about oat + CPAP, connected or unconnected is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":135},{"id":350,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP + weight loss — incremental reduction in insulin resistance, triglycerides and blood pressure versus either alone. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":139},{"id":353,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about oat + positional therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":140}],"count":13,"pdf_page":59},{"id":"3.9","title":"Comparative efficacy","text":"Separate physiological efficacy (reducing events while worn) from the patient-centred effects on symptoms, blood pressure and quality of life; neither a small AHI change nor high adherence proves all desired outcomes.","question_ids":[265,270,275,290,293,310,313,328,331],"examples":[{"id":265,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP lower · OAT higher · positional therapy similar to OAT. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":103},{"id":270,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. Which of the following statements about adherence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":105},{"id":275,"stem":"A patient has superior nightly use of a MAD despite a smaller per-hour AHI reduction than CPAP. Which concept best reconciles comparable outcomes?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":106},{"id":290,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP highest (~75%) · OAT moderate (~42%) · UPPP variable (~30%) · exercise and weight loss similar to MAD. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":113},{"id":293,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about AHI reduction is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":114},{"id":310,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: PAP typically has higher physiological suppression of AHI, while OAT can be better tolerated. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":122},{"id":313,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about pap versus oral appliance efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":123},{"id":328,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: OAT better for sleepiness; other measures similar in supine-dependent OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":129},{"id":331,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about versus positional therapy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":130}],"count":9,"pdf_page":60},{"id":"3.10","title":"Mean disease alleviation","text":"Mean disease alleviation is a conceptual function of efficacy and adherence. It explains why a less potent but more consistently used treatment can offer meaningful benefit; it is not a universal fixed percentage for every patient.","question_ids":[250,260,268,273,292,295,312,315,330,333],"examples":[{"id":250,"stem":"In a comparative trial, CPAP produces a larger reduction in AHI than a mandibular advancement device, but patients wear the oral appliance for longer. Which concept best reconciles these outcomes?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":98},{"id":260,"stem":"An appliance reduces untreated disease burden by 65% and is worn 80% of prescribed sleep time. What is the mean disease alleviation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":101},{"id":268,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: the overall percentage reduction in disease severity achieved in realworld conditions. Reflects true effectiveness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":104},{"id":273,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about mean disease alleviation = efficacy × compliance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":106},{"id":292,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":113},{"id":295,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":114},{"id":312,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: the extent to which patients correctly follow medical advice, often quantified as the percentage of prescribed therapy actually used. Determines how much of the efficacy is realised. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":122},{"id":315,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about compliance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":123},{"id":330,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: Physiological efficacy multiplied by the proportion of treatment actually used. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":129},{"id":333,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about mean disease alleviation calculation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":130}],"count":10,"pdf_page":60},{"id":"3.11","title":"Guidelines and evidence-based care","text":"The AASM/AADSM guideline favors a custom, titratable appliance over non-custom devices when OAT is prescribed for OSA and recommends follow-up testing and periodic reviews with both dentist and sleep provider.","question_ids":[271,276,278,294,297,314,317,332,335,342,345,352,355,358,361,368,370],"examples":[{"id":271,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: increments ≤1 mm · range ≥5 mm · reversible · verifiable. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":105},{"id":276,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about effective oa is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":107},{"id":278,"stem":"A new device has appealing advertising but no independently published efficacy data. Which principle should guide selection?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":107},{"id":294,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. The clinical team records the following finding: >50% AHI reduction and AHI <20; Friedman stage I best, III mostly unsuccessful; ~4% surgical adverse event rate. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":114},{"id":297,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about uppp success is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":115},{"id":314,"stem":"A 54-year-old with physician-confirmed moderate OSA is weighing treatment options. Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":123},{"id":317,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":124},{"id":332,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: provide appropriate care, reduce inappropriate care, increase effectiveness of care, by promoting best available evidence in decision-making. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":130},{"id":335,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about purpose of ebd is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":131},{"id":342,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP ~75% · OA ~42% · UPPP ~30%. MDA: OA ~51% vs CPAP ~50%. MAD 6.5 vs CPAP 5.2 h/night. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":135},{"id":345,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about AHI reduction is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":136},{"id":352,"stem":"An adult with hypertension and confirmed OSA requests an explanation of available therapies. The clinical team records the following finding: increments ≤1 mm · adjustment range ≥5 mm · reversible · verifiable setting. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":140},{"id":355,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about effective oa definition is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":141},{"id":358,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: 6 mm (limited protrusion <6 mm is a contraindication). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":142},{"id":361,"stem":"A sleep physician and dentist are discussing the most suitable treatment pathway. Which of the following statements about minimum protrusive distance for MAD is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":143},{"id":368,"stem":"A patient returning to the sleep clinic asks about the relative effects of different OSA treatments. The clinical team records the following finding: At most one-millimetre increments across a range of at least five millimetres. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":146},{"id":370,"stem":"A patient who finds continuous positive airway pressure difficult to tolerate asks about alternatives. Which of the following statements about effective mandibular appliance adjustment is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":147}],"count":17,"pdf_page":61}],"cases":[{"situation":"A patient with severe OSA refuses further CPAP after repeated mask adjustments and asks about an appliance.","decision":"Discuss custom titratable OAT with the treating medical provider, explain the possibility of residual disease and arrange objective verification.","why":"Severe OSA does not make the patient automatically ineligible, but the risk-benefit discussion and follow-up are crucial."},{"situation":"An adult wants a device for socially disruptive snoring but has never had OSA excluded.","decision":"Arrange appropriate assessment to establish primary snoring rather than dispensing a treatment directly.","why":"A history of snoring does not prove absence of clinically significant OSA."},{"situation":"A patient uses CPAP effectively but finds high pressures uncomfortable.","decision":"Consider medical PAP optimisation and, when suitable, multidisciplinary combination OAT/PAP.","why":"Switching straight to an unverified appliance may discard proven control."}],"pitfalls":["Do not convert study-level estimates such as “mean disease alleviation 50%” into a clinical success guarantee.","CPAP and OAT are not mutually exclusive in every patient; patient preference and objective outcomes are important."],"cue":"For a treatment comparison SBA, name the outcome first: AHI suppression, adherence, symptoms, comorbidity, or patient preference.","pages":{"id":3,"key":[47,48],"quick":[47,48],"detail":[49,65]},"count":125},{"id":4,"title":"History, dental examination, imaging and patient selection","short":"History, examination and imaging","lens":"Identify clinical suitability and modifiable risks without over-interpreting dental findings or incidental airway imaging.","sections":[{"id":"4.1","title":"History, objectives and examination","text":"Record the chief complaint, medical diagnosis, sleep-study summary, symptom burden, prior therapy, expectations and patient priorities. A dental sleep examination is broader than a mandibular protrusion measurement.","question_ids":[373,376,379,382,396,398,412,413,424,425,436,437,446,447,454,455,462,463,470],"examples":[{"id":373,"stem":"An older patient has six heavily restored teeth in each arch, mobile posterior teeth and untreated periodontitis. Which assessment is most important before designing a MAD?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":148},{"id":376,"stem":"A patient requests a mouthguard for severe tooth wear and also reports gasping during sleep. What should be addressed before choosing the splint?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":149},{"id":379,"stem":"Before a MAD is delivered, several posterior teeth show mobility and untreated periodontitis. What is the priority?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":150},{"id":382,"stem":"A clinician proposes CBCT solely to calculate a static airway volume before a MAD. Which response best matches the notes?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":151},{"id":396,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: attachment loss, mobility, prognosis of the teeth that will bear the appliance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":156},{"id":398,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about periodontal evaluation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":157},{"id":412,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: Occlusion — examine and record the existing condition; this is your medicolegal baseline. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":163},{"id":413,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about bruxism, orofacial pain, headaches is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":163},{"id":424,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about imaging is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":167},{"id":425,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about imaging is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":168},{"id":436,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: dental changes, discomfort — may occur. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":173},{"id":437,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about results vary, and some side effects is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":173},{"id":446,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: snoring, suspected or diagnosed OSA, sleep bruxism, or orofacial pain related to sleep disorders. Ask about sleep symptoms, previous diagnoses, comorbidities, and impact on daily life and well-being. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":177},{"id":447,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about reason for attendance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":178},{"id":454,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: modified Mallampati, tonsil grade, tongue size and scalloping, palatal vault, posterior pharyngeal crowding, signs of mouth breathing, signs of GORD such as enamel erosion, nasal patency. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":181},{"id":455,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about soft tissue/intraoral is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":182},{"id":462,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP, surgery, positional therapy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":184},{"id":463,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about explain the type of device being considered and all alternatives is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":185},{"id":470,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: age, weight, height; neck and belt circumference as indicated. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":187}],"count":19,"pdf_page":68},{"id":"4.2","title":"Medical and lifestyle modifiers","text":"Assess weight, alcohol use, tobacco, nasal obstruction, current medications and sleep schedule, because these can affect OSA control and the probability of using an appliance consistently.","question_ids":[377,394,399,401,414,415,426,427,438,439],"examples":[{"id":377,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about obesity is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":150},{"id":394,"stem":"A sleep patient takes sedatives, drinks alcohol at bedtime and has a BMI of 36. Which medical-history interpretation is best?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":155},{"id":399,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: anaesthetics, sedatives, opioids and neuromuscular blockers impair dilator function, increasing obstruction and respiratory failure risk, particularly perioperatively. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":157},{"id":401,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about drugs and medications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":158},{"id":414,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about smoking is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":163},{"id":415,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about smoking is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":164},{"id":426,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about alcohol is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":168},{"id":427,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about alcohol is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":168},{"id":438,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: chronic rhinosinusitis, allergic rhinitis, asthma or COPD worsen airway function and complicate management. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":173},{"id":439,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about other sleep disorders and comorbidities is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":174}],"count":10,"pdf_page":70},{"id":"4.3","title":"Other sleep disorders","text":"Ask about insomnia, restless legs, parasomnias and hypersomnolence disorders. Persistent fatigue after treatment does not by itself establish OAT failure or successful OSA control.","question_ids":[378,380,397,402,403,416,417,428,429,440,441,448,449,456,457,464,465],"examples":[{"id":378,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about the relationship appears mutual rather than cause-and-effect is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":150},{"id":380,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about the relationship appears mutual rather than cause-and-effect is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":151},{"id":397,"stem":"A person treated for OSA continues to have unrefreshing sleep and an urge to move the legs at night. What should be considered?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":156},{"id":402,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: May coexist, compounding sleepiness and cognitive impairment; symptom overlap makes clinical differentiation unreliable, so careful sleep studies are needed. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":158},{"id":403,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about narcolepsy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":158},{"id":416,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: tiredness, fatigue, sleepiness, poor motivation and poor concentration — complicating the diagnosis and treatment of each. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":164},{"id":417,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about symptoms of OSA and depression overlap is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":165},{"id":428,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: COMISA. Highly prevalent in SDB; the overlap produces more severe fragmentation, increased sleepiness and poorer quality of life. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":169},{"id":429,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about insomnia is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":169},{"id":440,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: SDB worsens both by further disrupting architecture, increasing cognitive and cardiovascular risk; shift workers with SDB are at higher risk of sleepiness and accidents. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":174},{"id":441,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about insufficient sleep and shift work disorder is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":175},{"id":448,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: SDB is associated with mood disorders, depression and anxiety, which may present with insomnia or hypersomnia, creating a cycle of worsening sleep and mental health. OSA and depression — the nuance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":178},{"id":449,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about mood disorders is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":179},{"id":456,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Frequently co-occur; SDB exacerbates limb movements and vice versa, causing further disruption and arousals; both contribute to sleepiness and impaired alertness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":182},{"id":457,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about rls and plmd is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":183},{"id":464,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: evidence is limited; some studies report benefit for both sleepiness and depression, others no effect. 4.4 Medical comorbidities. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":185},{"id":465,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about CPAP's effect on depression is not conclusive is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":185}],"count":17,"pdf_page":71},{"id":"4.4","title":"Medical comorbidities","text":"Cardiac and pulmonary disease, stroke, neuromuscular weakness, opioid exposure, pregnancy or major medical change may alter testing and care pathways. Coordinate with the patient’s medical providers.","question_ids":[371,381,383,404,405],"examples":[{"id":371,"stem":"A patient with OSA has resistant hypertension and type 2 diabetes. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":148},{"id":381,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: Higher rates particularly in women and post-menopausal populations. Neurocognitive decline — executive function, attention, memory — in adults and children, even at mild disease levels. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":151},{"id":383,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about depression, anxiety, neurocognitive decline is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":152},{"id":404,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: Hypertension, diabetes and metabolic syndrome are independently associated with moderate– severe SDB, and coexistence with obesity further increases risk. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":159},{"id":405,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about metabolic syndrome and diabetes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":159}],"count":5,"pdf_page":72},{"id":"4.5","title":"Ethics and patient consent","text":"Consent starts during planning: explain uncertainty of response, possible dental or bite changes, alternative treatments and the need for medical outcome verification.","question_ids":[384,386,400,406,407],"examples":[{"id":384,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Explain risks, benefits, alternatives including no treatment, and expected objective verification. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":152},{"id":386,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about informed dental treatment choice is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":153},{"id":400,"stem":"A patient values minimal dental side effects over complete numerical normalisation. What is the most appropriate planning approach?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":157},{"id":406,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Align the outcome with the patient and the physician, including symptom and objective disease endpoints. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":160},{"id":407,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about patient-specific goals is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":160}],"count":5,"pdf_page":72},{"id":"4.6","title":"Multidisciplinary communication","text":"Document diagnosis source, treatment plan, shared decisions and written communication between dentist, medical provider and general dentist when relevant. Define who is responsible for follow-up testing.","question_ids":[374,385,387,389,408,409,418,419,430,431],"examples":[{"id":374,"stem":"A patient asks whether the dentist alone can confirm that a new MAD has eliminated their severe OSA. What is the best team-based reply?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":149},{"id":385,"stem":"A patient presents with a diagnostic PSG but no physician treatment communication. What should the dentist obtain?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":152},{"id":387,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: Adult mild 5–14 · moderate 15–30 · severe >30. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":153},{"id":389,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about severity and outcome in the shared-care setting is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":154},{"id":408,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Level 3 = ≥4 channels including airflow (≥2 channels of respiratory movement, or movement and airflow), heart rate/ECG, oxygen saturation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":161},{"id":409,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about portable monitor levels is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":161},{"id":418,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about shared is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":165},{"id":419,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about shared is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":165},{"id":430,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Primary snoring without sleepiness → still refer to exclude OSA before providing an appliance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":170},{"id":431,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about borderline cases is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":170}],"count":10,"pdf_page":72},{"id":"4.7","title":"Sleep bruxism","text":"Sleep bruxism may coexist with OSA but does not diagnose it. A protective splint is not an OSA treatment; consider respiratory symptoms before fitting a device that changes mandibular position.","question_ids":[372,388,390,392,410,411,420,421,432,433,442,443,450,451,458,459,466,467],"examples":[{"id":372,"stem":"A patient with significant tooth wear reports frequent grinding, snoring and witnessed respiratory pauses. The patient asks for an occlusal splint. What should the dentist do first?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":148},{"id":388,"stem":"A patient with both sleep bruxism and OSA asks whether one causes the other. What is the best answer?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":153},{"id":390,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: 6–91%, depending on assessment method. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":154},{"id":392,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about wide reported range is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":155},{"id":410,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: SB is not a disorder but a behaviour that may be a risk factor or a protective factor for clinical consequences. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":162},{"id":411,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about corollary is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":162},{"id":420,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: a masticatory muscle activity during sleep, characterised as rhythmic (phasic — grinding) or non-rhythmic (tonic — clenching), and not a movement disorder or a sleep disorder in otherwise healthy individuals. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":166},{"id":421,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about sleep bruxism is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":166},{"id":432,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Sequence: muscle tone falls → obstruction → more negative intraluminal pressure → bruxism triggered, increasing tone and restoring patency → less negative pressure. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":171},{"id":433,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about the \"reactive–protective\" hypothesis is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":171},{"id":442,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: possible (self-report), probable (self-report + clinical examination), definite (self-report + examination + instrumental assessment, notably PSG with EMG). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":175},{"id":443,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. Which of the following statements about assessment grading is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":176},{"id":450,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: assess risk factors including behavioural; treat the TMD (often self-limiting, non-surgical) and avoid a MAD until the TMD is treated. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":179},{"id":451,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about sb + TMD is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":180},{"id":458,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: ~8% of the adult population. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":183},{"id":459,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about self-reported sleep bruxism is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":183},{"id":466,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: both commoner supine; both involve oropharyngeal and masticatory muscle activation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":186},{"id":467,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about shared clinical features is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":186}],"count":18,"pdf_page":75},{"id":"4.8","title":"Pain, TMD and sleep","text":"TMD is assessed clinically and managed proportionately. Pain or limited opening may constrain OAT design or titration, but a past TMD diagnosis does not always exclude OAT.","question_ids":[375,391,393,395,422,423,434,435,444,445,452,453,460,461,468,469],"examples":[{"id":375,"stem":"A patient with controlled, mild TMD and physician-confirmed OSA is being considered for a custom MAD. What is the most appropriate conclusion?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":149},{"id":391,"stem":"A patient with TMD is being considered for oral appliance therapy. Which assessment is essential?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":154},{"id":393,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: pain ≥3 months + ≥11 of 18 tender sites at 4 kg; prevalence 1–2%, 80% female. CFS 0.4–1.5%. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":155},{"id":395,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about fibromyalgia (acr 1990) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":156},{"id":422,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: hyperalgesia — short or disturbed sleep increases sensitivity to painful stimulation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":167},{"id":423,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about conversely is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":167},{"id":434,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: chronic pain management must address sleep simultaneously, usually multidisciplinary; psychological input for the mood and anxiety components; improving sleep improves pain tolerance and quality of life. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":172},{"id":435,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about implications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":172},{"id":444,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. The clinical team records the following finding: dysregulated serotonin, noradrenaline and dopamine (regulating both pain and sleep); pro-inflammatory TNF-α and IL-6 in both pathways; HPA-axis dysfunction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":176},{"id":445,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. Which of the following statements about mechanisms is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":177},{"id":452,"stem":"A 46-year-old with OSA is referred for dental suitability assessment before oral appliance therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: reported range 6–91%; self-reported adult ~8%; children and adolescents 14–46%; 30–50% of OSA pa- tients. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":180},{"id":453,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. Which of the following statements about sb prevalence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":181},{"id":460,"stem":"A 59-year-old with dental restorations and OSA asks about eligibility for mandibular advancement. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI/RDI/REI 50% reduction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":184},{"id":461,"stem":"A patient with facial pain and unrefreshing sleep presents for multidisciplinary assessment. Which of the following statements about successful treatment outcome is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":184},{"id":468,"stem":"A patient with tooth wear, jaw clicking and disruptive snoring attends an initial dental assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI/RDI/REI 50% reduction. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":187},{"id":469,"stem":"A dentist is reviewing the medical and dental history of a new oral appliance candidate. Which of the following statements about successful outcome is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":187}],"count":16,"pdf_page":78}],"cases":[{"situation":"A patient seeks a night guard for severe wear and reports gasping at night.","decision":"Screen for SDB and refer for diagnosis before making assumptions about the effect of a flat-plane splint.","why":"Tooth wear alone cannot identify the respiratory cause or the best appliance."},{"situation":"CBCT shows a small airway in an otherwise asymptomatic patient.","decision":"Interpret the image only in its proper dental context and use symptoms/risk factors for sleep referral decisions.","why":"Static airway volume on CBCT cannot diagnose the dynamic collapse occurring during sleep."},{"situation":"A patient with a history of TMD has mild jaw discomfort after trial protrusion.","decision":"Assess baseline symptoms and adjust pace/design; treat active pain and review rather than labelling any discomfort an absolute contraindication.","why":"Tolerance is individual, and progressive pain requires a different response from transient adaptation."}],"pitfalls":["Do not claim that cephalometry, photographs or CBCT alone diagnoses or grades OSA.","Do not equate “bruxism and snoring together” with a proven causal relationship."],"cue":"A good examination answer states what history changes management, what the dentist can assess, and when medical referral is needed.","pages":{"id":4,"key":[66,67],"quick":[66,67],"detail":[68,81]},"count":100},{"id":5,"title":"Appliance design, registration, manufacture and fitting","short":"Appliance selection, construction and fitting","lens":"Select and calibrate a durable appliance around the patient’s dental condition rather than chasing a memorised protrusion percentage.","sections":[{"id":"5.1","title":"Clinical guideline and diagnosis","text":"Before providing OAT, confirm the medical diagnosis and the agreed treatment pathway. The 2025 AADSM standards emphasise qualified dental assessment and in-person comprehensive examination, impressions and construction-bite records.","question_ids":[479,502,504,517,520,529,532,539,542,549,552],"examples":[{"id":479,"stem":"A patient seeks an appliance for 'primary snoring' without any medical assessment. What is required first?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":190},{"id":502,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: greater effectiveness demonstrated. Upper airway surgery (tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, craniofacial operations, tracheostomy) may also supersede appliance use where predicted to be highly effective. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":198},{"id":504,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about severe OSA should have an initial trial of nasal CPAP is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":199},{"id":517,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: generally ≥6 teeth per arch (stricter: 8–10 sound teeth per arch with adequate bone support); assess mobility, spacing, curve of Spee, restorative status. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":205},{"id":520,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about dentition is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":206},{"id":529,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: Primary snoring without OSA or UARS: reduce snoring to a subjectively acceptable level. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":211},{"id":532,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about treatment objectives is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":212},{"id":539,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: nasolaryngoscopy to evaluate pharyngeal stenosis through the naso-, velo-, oroand laryngopharynx, with jaw thrust and Müller manoeuvre. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":216},{"id":542,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about nasopharyngeal assessment is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":217},{"id":549,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: Custom titratable devices reduce AHI, arousal index and ODI and increase oxygen saturation to a greater extent. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":220},{"id":552,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about use a custom, titratable appliance over non-custom devices is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":222}],"count":11,"pdf_page":84},{"id":"5.2","title":"Upper-airway mechanisms and predictors","text":"Airway response is variable: anatomy, compliance, mandibular range, vertical opening and comfort matter. A larger protrusion is not invariably better if it creates pain or prevents all-night use.","question_ids":[477,478,505,506,519,522,541,544,551,554,559,562,569,572,579,582,587,590,593,596,599],"examples":[{"id":477,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about mild OSA suits the mechanism better is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":190},{"id":478,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: less collapsible airways, and the pharynx increases more along its whole length than in severe disease. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":190},{"id":505,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: sufficient AHI reduction on an overnight test with a remotely controlled mandibular positioner — the best predictor available (\"excellent\"), against clinical/craniofacial prediction (\"poor\") and nasopharyngoscopy (\"fair to…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":199},{"id":506,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about trial-based is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":200},{"id":519,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about dose dependence extends beyond anatomy is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":206},{"id":522,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about dose dependence extends beyond anatomy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":207},{"id":541,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: supine-position-dependent (positional) OSA; NREM-predominant or non-stage-dependent rather than REM-predominant. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":217},{"id":544,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about polysomnographic is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":218},{"id":551,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: particularly in its lateral dimension at the velopharyngeal level — and the tongue is displaced anteriorly; velopharyngeal, nasopharyngeal and anteroposterior dimensions all increase. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":221},{"id":554,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about the airway enlarges is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":223},{"id":559,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: younger; female; lower BMI and non-obese; smaller neck circumference; lower baseline AHI. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":225},{"id":562,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about patient traits is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":226},{"id":569,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: less collapsible airway; lower loop gain; higher arousal threshold; lower response to arousal. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":229},{"id":572,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about endotype is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":230},{"id":579,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about adverse is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":233},{"id":582,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about adverse is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":234},{"id":587,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: Advancement reduces collapsibility dosedependently without systematically changing genioglossus function, in a predominantly obese, severe-OSA population. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":237},{"id":590,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about primary mode of action is improvement in passive pharyngeal anatomy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":238},{"id":593,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: shorter soft palate; longer maxilla; lower hyoid and decreased hyoid-to-mandibular-plane distance; greater angle between cranial base and mandibular plane; retrognathic mandible with narrow SNB and wider SNA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":240},{"id":596,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about cephalometric is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":241},{"id":599,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: patient factors alone are not a good predictor, and demographic, anthropometric and polysomnographic data only weakly inform efficacy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":243}],"count":21,"pdf_page":87},{"id":"5.3","title":"Indications and contraindications","text":"Assess attachment and retention: periodontal support, remaining teeth, restorations, prostheses, oral hygiene, TMJ function and patient dexterity. A minimum tooth count quoted in notes is a heuristic, not a standalone eligibility rule.","question_ids":[480,481,482,507,508],"examples":[{"id":480,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about severe OSA is a relative contraindication only is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":191},{"id":481,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP should be trialled first, but an appliance is appropriate if CPAP fails. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":191},{"id":482,"stem":"A patient has advanced active periodontal disease with mobile anchor teeth. Which appliance decision is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":191},{"id":507,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about indications is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":200},{"id":508,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":200}],"count":5,"pdf_page":88},{"id":"5.4","title":"Device designs and materials","text":"Choose an effective, appropriately regulated custom adjustable device, weighing bulk, durability, repairability, adjustability, material tolerance and lateral movement. Avoid design selection by marketing claims alone.","question_ids":[473,483,494,510,521,524,531,534,543,546,553,556,561,564,571,574,581,584,589,592,595,598],"examples":[{"id":473,"stem":"A patient with a history of dental restorations requires a mandibular advancement device that can be titrated gradually. Which appliance property is most important?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":188},{"id":483,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about selection factors is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":192},{"id":494,"stem":"An appliance cannot be adjusted after manufacture and has no verifiable setting. What is the key problem?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":195},{"id":510,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about mono/twin block is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":201},{"id":521,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: dual posts from both overlays interlocking perpendicularly to guide, reposition and stabilise the mandible. Titrated by swapping an overlay for one with a different setting (e.g. +1 mm). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":207},{"id":524,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about precision post is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":208},{"id":531,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: The influence of design on efficacy and side effects is almost unknown, and side effects are a product of protruding the mandible to achieve a therapeutic effect rather than of specific design. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":212},{"id":534,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about between-device differences are clinically small in symptoms or AHI is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":213},{"id":543,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about not recommended is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":218},{"id":546,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about not recommended is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":219},{"id":553,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: good mechanical properties, well established; retention by frictional resistance; can be relined and repaired; used in DLP and SLA printing. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":222},{"id":556,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about acrylic (pmma) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":223},{"id":561,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: angled dorsal posts from the mandibular overlay interlocking with maxillary blocks. Titrated by screws in the blocks (e.g. 10 turns = +1 mm). An unattached mechanism. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":226},{"id":564,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about interlocking dorsal is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":227},{"id":571,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: dental casts and bite registrations used to create custom appliances. Well established, allowing precise custom fitting to the patient's anatomy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":230},{"id":574,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about traditional is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":231},{"id":581,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: digital models built layer by layer. High customisation, potentially reduced production time and cost, and in-house printing offering fast replacement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":234},{"id":584,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about 3d printing / additive manufacturing is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":235},{"id":589,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: lighter, smaller, robust, less breakage so better for bruxers; retention by flexion into undercuts; needs only ~3 mm clearance; titration scale visible on the device. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":238},{"id":592,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about nylon / polyamide (pa12), cad-cam is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":239},{"id":595,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: bilateral elastomeric or nylon straps pulling the mandibular overlay forward. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":241},{"id":598,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about lateral pull is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":242}],"count":22,"pdf_page":89},{"id":"5.5","title":"Individual device selection","text":"Individual selection should reflect oral anatomy, bruxism, gag reflex, mouth breathing, need for dental work, dexterity, cost and patient preference. Document why a particular design is chosen.","question_ids":[484,486,509,512,523,526,533,536,545,548,555,558,563,566,573,576],"examples":[{"id":484,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: assess and treat first. It does not itself treat the OSA but improves the treatment outcome. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":192},{"id":486,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about nasal patency is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":193},{"id":509,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: class II vs III alters mechanics and the direction of unwanted movement. An anterior open bite precludes a MAD — no posterior clearance at all. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":201},{"id":512,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about arch relationship is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":202},{"id":523,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: sufficient healthy teeth and a suitable bite are crucial. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":208},{"id":526,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about dental factors is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":209},{"id":533,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: perceived comfort, ease of use, durability, cost. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":213},{"id":536,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about patient preference is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":214},{"id":545,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: severe maxillomandibular deformity may need specialised treatment; retrognathia favours an appliance and is a specific problem for CPAP because of mask leakage. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":218},{"id":548,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about skeletal pattern is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":220},{"id":555,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: a TRD is an option for missing teeth or where jaw alignment precludes a MAD. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":223},{"id":558,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about mouth and tongue is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":224},{"id":563,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: an anterior adjustment mechanism suits poor dexterity, accepting anterior force concentration. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":227},{"id":566,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about dexterity, cognition, visual acuity, range of motion is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":228},{"id":573,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: severity and positional dependence; adjustable MADs have higher response rates than fixed devices. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":231},{"id":576,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about sleep study findings is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":232}],"count":16,"pdf_page":92},{"id":"5.6","title":"Construction bite registration","text":"Take a reproducible construction-bite registration in the patient’s tolerable protrusive range and record the position. There is no universally best starting percentage; incremental adjustment should balance efficacy and comfort.","question_ids":[472,485,487,488,489,491,511,514,525,528,535,538,547,550,557,560,565,568,575,578,583,586],"examples":[{"id":472,"stem":"A patient has a maximum protrusive range of 10 mm and is being registered for a custom titratable MAD. Which initial registration is the most defensible?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":188},{"id":485,"stem":"Maximum measured protrusive range is 10 mm. A cautious initial construction bite is planned at 50% of range. What advancement is registered?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":192},{"id":487,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: uses speech (phonation of particular sounds) to find a position that keeps the airway open without excessive protrusion or opening. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":193},{"id":488,"stem":"A patient develops discomfort when the construction bite is held at near-maximum protrusion. What adjustment is best justified?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":193},{"id":489,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about phonetic is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":194},{"id":491,"stem":"Two otherwise equivalent devices differ mainly in vertical opening. Which choice aligns with the notebook?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":194},{"id":511,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: the principal therapeutic variable. Start at 50–70% of maximum protrusion; AADSM 2025 states 25–75% lies within both the comfort and therapeutic range. Do not exceed ~75% initially. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":202},{"id":514,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about horizontal (protrusion) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":203},{"id":525,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: bite gauges and mandibular positioning simulators for reproducibility. Digital/ virtual — scanning and CAD, potentially outperforming traditional methods in accuracy and efficiency. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":209},{"id":528,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about clinical measurement and simulation tools is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":210},{"id":535,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation relaxes the muscles, allowing a relaxed physiological mandibular position before recording, reducing nociceptive input and potentially improving accuracy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":214},{"id":538,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about neuromuscular (tens) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":215},{"id":547,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: accommodates asymmetry or deviation. The midline should be reproducible at both centric and protrusion; it need not be identical, but it must be reproducible. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":219},{"id":550,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about lateral (midline) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":221},{"id":557,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. The clinical team records the following finding: mechanical devices giving precise control and measurement in three dimensions, with guides and rulers documenting advancement and vertical opening numerically. The George Gauge is the archetype. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":224},{"id":560,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about construction bite devices is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":225},{"id":565,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: provides interocclusal space without discomfort or fatigue. ~2 mm interincisal (moderate 1–3 mm increase typical). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":228},{"id":568,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about vertical (bite opening) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":229},{"id":575,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: The acceptable range for an individual is not known. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":232},{"id":578,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about why not maximum protrusion is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":233},{"id":583,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: occlusal registration at 60% of maximum mandibular protrusion with 2 mm of vertical separation of the upper and lower incisors. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":235},{"id":586,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about classical practice is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":236}],"count":22,"pdf_page":93},{"id":"5.7","title":"Impression accuracy","text":"Capture accurate dentition and soft tissue boundaries with an appropriate impression or intraoral scan. Inadequate detail can cause poor fit, pressure spots and unretentive appliances.","question_ids":[471,490,492],"examples":[{"id":471,"stem":"A lab says the impression tore at a retentive molar undercut. Which material property is most relevant?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":188},{"id":490,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about requirements is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":194},{"id":492,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about requirements is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":195}],"count":3,"pdf_page":94},{"id":"5.8","title":"Laboratory prescription","text":"Send the laboratory a clear prescription describing design, material, bite position, retention, permitted movements, undercuts and relevant clinical cautions; confirm the delivered device matches the prescription.","question_ids":[476,493,495,497],"examples":[{"id":476,"stem":"A laboratory produces a device at an arbitrary mandibular advancement because the prescription lacked a construction bite. What error occurred?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":189},{"id":493,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: Specify the chosen design, impressions or scans, bite registration, advancement and vertical settings. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":195},{"id":495,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about laboratory prescription contents is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":196},{"id":497,"stem":"A dental laboratory receives an appliance order without the protrusive registration, design or material. What is the best corrective action?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":196}],"count":4,"pdf_page":95},{"id":"5.9","title":"Appliance delivery","text":"At delivery check insertion/removal, stable retention, margins, tissue contact, vertical dimension and protrusion setting. Demonstrate how to adjust and what symptoms should prompt review.","question_ids":[475,496,498,500,513,516],"examples":[{"id":475,"stem":"A patient attends the delivery appointment for a new custom mandibular advancement appliance. Which is the most important initial clinical check?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":189},{"id":496,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: Assess complete seating, patient-removable retention, stability, comfort and demonstrated insertion/removal. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":196},{"id":498,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. Which of the following statements about delivery check is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":197},{"id":500,"stem":"At delivery a mandibular device dislodges easily when the patient opens. What should the dentist assess first?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":197},{"id":513,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: Teach insertion, removal, cleaning, adjustment and the need for further appointments. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":203},{"id":516,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about device instruction at fit is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":204}],"count":6,"pdf_page":96},{"id":"5.10","title":"Home care and maintenance","text":"Give written instructions for cleaning, storage, adjustment limits, morning bite assessment, breakage and follow-up. Patient comprehension is part of the intervention, not an optional extra.","question_ids":[474,499,501,503,515,518,527,530,537,540,567,570,577,580,585,588,591,594,597,600],"examples":[{"id":474,"stem":"A new MAD user asks about daily cleaning and storage. Which advice is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":189},{"id":499,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: 2 mm grey George Gauge fork (5 mm white fork usually too much); interincisal increase 2–4 mm; posterior clearance nylon ~3 mm, acrylic ~5 mm, some designs 6 mm. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":197},{"id":501,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. Which of the following statements about vertical is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":198},{"id":503,"stem":"A patient reports cleaning a nylon appliance with hot water and abrasive paste. What advice is appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":198},{"id":515,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: Brush and floss regularly. Morning routine: gentle jaw exercises — resting the chin on a fist, curling the tongue back to find the retruded position — and the morning occlusal guide for 15–20 minutes if…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":204},{"id":518,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about oral health is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":205},{"id":527,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: Remove over a soft surface or a basin of water. Air dry before placing in the ventilated case. Store dry in the case — never in tissue or loose in a pocket. Keep away from sunlight, heat, pets and children. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":210},{"id":530,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about handling and storage is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":211},{"id":537,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: dentition + ~3 mm gingiva; detail to 20–25 µm; acrylic plate 1.5–2 mm thick. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":215},{"id":540,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about impression is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":216},{"id":567,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: every 6 months in year 1, then at least annually. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":228},{"id":570,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about follow-up is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":229},{"id":577,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: ≥6 teeth per arch (stricter: 8–10 sound teeth per arch with adequate bone support). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":232},{"id":580,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about dentition is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":233},{"id":585,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. The clinical team records the following finding: 25–75% acceptable); start at 50%, do not exceed 75%; the challenged \"70% threshold\" concept. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":236},{"id":588,"stem":"A patient with several crowns is being considered for mandibular advancement therapy. Which of the following statements about initial protrusion 50–70% of maximum (aadsm 2025 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":237},{"id":591,"stem":"At appliance delivery, a patient reports a comfortable initial protrusive setting. The clinical team records the following finding: absence predicts better response. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":239},{"id":594,"stem":"A follow-up patient reports poor retention of a recently supplied oral appliance. Which of the following statements about pterygomandibular raphe present in ~64% of people is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":240},{"id":597,"stem":"A dentist and laboratory technician discuss the design of a custom titratable oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: Rinse immediately after removal. Clean each morning with a soft-bristled brush and mild soap or denture cleaner, using cool or warm — never hot — water (hot water warps the device). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":242},{"id":600,"stem":"A 52-year-old with diagnosed OSA attends the construction-bite appointment for a custom MAD. Which of the following statements about cleaning is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":243}],"count":20,"pdf_page":97}],"cases":[{"situation":"A patient with mobile posterior teeth and severe periodontal breakdown requests a rigid appliance.","decision":"Stabilise dental disease and reconsider retention/design or alternative therapy before fabrication.","why":"An arbitrary “six teeth per arch” rule cannot replace the biomechanical assessment of anchorage."},{"situation":"A high-protrusion construction bite causes TMJ pain during registration.","decision":"Record a more tolerable starting position and plan gradual reassessment and medical efficacy verification.","why":"Forcing maximum advancement may undermine adherence and cause harm."},{"situation":"The delivered device is bulky, unstable and prevents comfortable closure.","decision":"Correct fit/design before starting titration and document defects and changes.","why":"Titrating a poorly fitted device does not correct its fundamental delivery problem."}],"pitfalls":["Avoid claiming that 50%, 70%, or 75% maximum protrusion applies to every patient; distinguish an illustrative starting protocol from an obligatory rule.","Vertical opening, tooth count and appliance durability are patient/design-specific, not universal binary thresholds."],"cue":"The strongest device-choice SBA answer balances retention, tolerability, adjustability, adherence and expected airway response.","pages":{"id":5,"key":[82,83],"quick":[82,83],"detail":[84,100]},"count":130},{"id":6,"title":"Titration, treatment effectiveness and adherence","short":"Titration, effectiveness and adherence","lens":"Optimise a therapeutic position without mistaking comfort or a quieter partner for proven disease control.","sections":[{"id":"6.1","title":"Chief complaint and adjustment","text":"Return to the original complaint and the baseline sleep study. Ask whether the patient’s snoring, sleepiness, awakenings and functional goals improved, and whether adherence supports a fair treatment trial.","question_ids":[601,622,623,627,640,641,645,656,657,666,667,676,677],"examples":[{"id":601,"stem":"A patient reports less snoring two weeks after MAD fitting but has worsening bilateral jaw soreness. What is the best titration decision?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":244},{"id":622,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. Which of the following statements about initial protrusive range is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":251},{"id":623,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. The clinical team records the following finding: Start commonly around 50% of the measured maximum advancement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":251},{"id":627,"stem":"A baseline ESS was 16 but the dentist changes to a different questionnaire at follow-up. What is lost?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":252},{"id":640,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. Which of the following statements about early appointment timing is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":257},{"id":641,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. The clinical team records the following finding: Reassess within 30 days of insertion. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":257},{"id":645,"stem":"At three weeks a MAD user asks how much to advance per turn. Which principle best limits early discomfort?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":258},{"id":656,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about why revert is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":262},{"id":657,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: to resolve unacceptable side effects; to balance efficacy against tolerance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":262},{"id":666,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about patient-led adjustment window is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":266},{"id":667,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: About six weeks of gradual self-adjustment may follow acclimatisation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":266},{"id":676,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about typical overall titration time is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":270},{"id":677,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: Approximately two to five months. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":270}],"count":13,"pdf_page":103},{"id":"6.2","title":"Symptoms and side-effects monitoring","text":"At every adjustment, review oral discomfort, jaw pain, occlusal change, retention and device function. A patient may be improving symptomatically while side effects are eroding their ability to use the appliance.","question_ids":[607,608,621,625,626,643,644,651,658,659,668,669,678,679],"examples":[{"id":607,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. Which of the following statements about dental discomfort and bite change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":246},{"id":608,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. The clinical team records the following finding: tooth pain, gingival irritation, soreness, the bite feeling \"off\" or teeth not fitting as before; long-term use decreases overbite and overjet — monitor and record. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":246},{"id":621,"stem":"A patient says the bite feels 'off' for an hour after removing the splint. Which question is most useful?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":250},{"id":625,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about unexpected persistent snoring is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":252},{"id":626,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: Reassess adherence, appliance stability, symptom change and residual OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":252},{"id":643,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about jaw and tmj is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":258},{"id":644,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: discomfort, stiffness, pain, clicking or sounds; usually improve, but persistent or severe symptoms need management or adjustment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":258},{"id":651,"stem":"A patient develops a sore gingival margin without recurrent snoring. What is the immediate device-focused assessment?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":260},{"id":658,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about appliance condition is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":263},{"id":659,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: cracks, warping, wear affecting fit and efficacy; confirm retention and stability. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":263},{"id":668,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about salivation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":266},{"id":669,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: excessive salivation or dry mouth, common initially and often transient (salivation typically settles after a few weeks) but document and manage. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":267},{"id":678,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about tolerance and adherence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":270},{"id":679,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: comfort with nightly use, and whether side effects are reducing use. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":271}],"count":14,"pdf_page":104},{"id":"6.3","title":"Referral for objective assessment","text":"The treating medical provider should verify efficacy objectively after the therapeutic position is established. Additional medical testing is warranted when symptoms recur, disease risk changes or residual disease is suspected.","question_ids":[602,606,610,611,628,629,633,646,647],"examples":[{"id":602,"stem":"After three months using a MAD, a patient reports much less snoring but remains sleepy. What is the best way to establish whether OSA is adequately controlled?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":244},{"id":606,"stem":"A patient feels dramatically better using a MAD; baseline AHI was 38. How should treatment success be confirmed?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":245},{"id":610,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. Which of the following statements about not indicated is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":247},{"id":611,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. The clinical team records the following finding: primary snoring, unless symptoms worsen or fail to resolve. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":247},{"id":628,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. Which of the following statements about who verifies efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":253},{"id":629,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. The clinical team records the following finding: The treating physician using appropriate objective sleep testing. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":253},{"id":633,"stem":"A patient has residual AHI 17 after comfortable titration. Who should determine whether the remaining disease is acceptably controlled?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":254},{"id":646,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. Which of the following statements about dentist-operated portable data is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":259},{"id":647,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. The clinical team records the following finding: Used only to guide titration within local scope rules. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":259}],"count":9,"pdf_page":105},{"id":"6.4","title":"Clinical and instrumented titration","text":"Incremental titration aims for the most effective tolerable mandibular position. A symptom-guided trial and objective data may both inform adjustment; excessive protrusion is not automatically a stronger treatment.","question_ids":[605,609,613,614,624,631,632,636,648,649,650,660,661,670,671,680],"examples":[{"id":605,"stem":"A patient is comfortable at 5 mm MAD advancement but develops persistent jaw pain at 7 mm. Which is the best next step?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":245},{"id":609,"stem":"A mandibular positioner identifies an effective setting during an overnight study. What is the next appropriate step?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":246},{"id":613,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about multimethod positioning is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":248},{"id":614,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: initial positioning → resolution or improvement of signs and symptoms → pulse oximetry, highresolution oximetry or HSAT administered by the qualified dentist, and/or HSAT or PSG ordered by the physician → verification of…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":248},{"id":624,"stem":"After gradual protrusion the patient develops severe unilateral TMJ pain. What is best done?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":251},{"id":631,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about further titration, modification, redesign or remake as required is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":254},{"id":632,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: Monitor monthly for the first six months and at least annually thereafter for as long as the appliance is worn. 6.5 Efficacy, long-term effectiveness, limitations. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":254},{"id":636,"stem":"A 55-year-old is comfortable at 5 mm advancement but develops jaw pain at 7 mm. What is the most appropriate therapeutic target?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":255},{"id":648,"stem":"On a treatment study, the patient removes the appliance midway through recording because of pain. What should the report include?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":259},{"id":649,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. Which of the following statements about both end in the same place is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":260},{"id":650,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. The clinical team records the following finding: The dentist's objective data supplement the titration decision; they never replace physician verification. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":260},{"id":660,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. Which of the following statements about signs and symptoms only is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":263},{"id":661,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. The clinical team records the following finding: initial mandibular positioning → resolution or improvement of signs and symptoms → verification of efficacy by the physician. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":264},{"id":670,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. Which of the following statements about drug-induced sleep endoscopy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":267},{"id":671,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. The clinical team records the following finding: a remotely controlled positioner can be used with DISE to titrate mandibular position under direct visualisation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":268},{"id":680,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. The clinical team records the following finding: allows objective, single-night titration during polysomnography to identify the optimal therapeutic advancement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":271}],"count":16,"pdf_page":106},{"id":"6.5","title":"Efficacy and effectiveness","text":"Distinguish complete disease control from a partial AHI response and from a meaningful but incomplete patient benefit. Explain residual AHI, oxygenation and symptoms separately rather than relying on a single “success percentage.”","question_ids":[603,612,616,617,630,634,635,642,652,653,662,663,672,673],"examples":[{"id":603,"stem":"A patient has a large relative AHI improvement after OAT but the residual AHI remains above the normal range. Which is the best conclusion?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":244},{"id":612,"stem":"A patient's snoring resolves but follow-up AHI remains 24. Which statement is most accurate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":247},{"id":616,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. Which of the following statements about persistent snoring, no other symptoms is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":249},{"id":617,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. The clinical team records the following finding: may be acceptable to the patient; but persistent snoring on treatment relates to insufficient apnoea control and poor adherence, so verify objectively before accepting it. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":249},{"id":630,"stem":"A patient has 50% AHI reduction from 40 to 20 but unchanged daytime symptoms. Which conclusion is most defensible?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":253},{"id":634,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. Which of the following statements about partial treatment response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":255},{"id":635,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. The clinical team records the following finding: Improved symptoms with at least a 50% AHI fall but residual AHI at least five. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":255},{"id":642,"stem":"A patient reaches AHI 4 but continues to report major sleepiness. How should the dentist respond?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":257},{"id":652,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about complete treatment response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":261},{"id":653,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: Resolution of symptoms and AHI less than five per hour on treatment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":261},{"id":662,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about long-term effectiveness is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":264},{"id":663,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: Health outcomes are comparable to CPAP at least short-term, even in severe OSA, with greater adherence to the splint; appliances also benefit blood pressure, endothelial function and left ventricular function. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":265},{"id":672,"stem":"A dentist is titrating a custom appliance while monitoring discomfort and adherence. Which of the following statements about ongoing clinical failure is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":268},{"id":673,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: Persistent symptoms with insufficient objective improvement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":269}],"count":14,"pdf_page":107},{"id":"6.6","title":"Adherence measurement","text":"Distinguish self-reported nightly wear from objective adherence sensors when available. Assessment of efficacy requires consideration of actual hours and nights of appliance use.","question_ids":[604,615,618,619,620,637,638,639,654,655,664,665,674,675],"examples":[{"id":604,"stem":"A patient reports wearing a MAD nightly for eight hours, but the objective wear-time sensor shows much shorter use. Which conclusion is most defensible?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":245},{"id":615,"stem":"An oral appliance appears ineffective but a temperature microsensor shows it is worn two hours nightly. What should be addressed?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":248},{"id":618,"stem":"A patient wears a MAD for 6 hours on three nights a week. Does this meet the notebook's AADSM adherence definition?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":249},{"id":619,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about it distinguishes device inefficacy from poor compliance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":250},{"id":620,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: the single most important diagnostic function of adherence data. Without it you cannot know whether to advance the appliance or address behaviour. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":250},{"id":637,"stem":"A patient with previously moderate OSA has reached the maximally comfortable protrusion. Which of the following statements about titration is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":256},{"id":638,"stem":"A sleep specialist receives an update on an oral appliance candidate during titration. The clinical team records the following finding: start at 50% of maximum; 2-week acclimatisation, then 6-week self-adjustment; increments 0.2–0.4 mm per night; total titration ~2–5 months; vertical dimension held constant. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":256},{"id":639,"stem":"A patient reports wearing the appliance every night, but sensor data show use only twice a week. What is the key clinical consequence?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":256},{"id":654,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about aadsm compliance is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":261},{"id":655,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: ≥80% of the night, ≥5 nights per week, with ≥7 h sleep. CPAP convention ≥4 h per night. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":262},{"id":664,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":265},{"id":665,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. Which of the following statements about efficacy is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":265},{"id":674,"stem":"A patient returns after several weeks of MAD use and says snoring has improved, but fatigue persists. Which of the following statements about recorded adherence technology is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":269},{"id":675,"stem":"A 55-year-old feels better after appliance adjustment and asks whether treatment is now effective. The clinical team records the following finding: A device-embedded temperature sensor can objectively estimate nightly wear. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":269}],"count":14,"pdf_page":108}],"cases":[{"situation":"A patient reports no snoring after two months of OAT but retains AHI 22/h on treatment testing.","decision":"Treat this as incomplete objectively verified control and confer with the sleep provider about further titration or alternatives.","why":"Absence of snoring is not equivalent to satisfactory OSA treatment."},{"situation":"During titration, the AHI improves slightly, but each additional advancement causes progressive jaw pain.","decision":"Do not continue indefinite advancement; reassess tolerance and other therapy options.","why":"An improved index is of little use if pain prevents sustained treatment."},{"situation":"A patient claims nightly adherence yet the embedded sensor records use on two nights per week.","decision":"Address barriers to usage before declaring the appliance biologically ineffective.","why":"Low adherence confounds efficacy assessment and mean disease alleviation."}],"pitfalls":["A patient who chooses “reveal answer” in the web question bank has not demonstrated a correct response; the scoring system separately records unanswered and revealed-only.","Symptom resolution, 50% AHI reduction and AHI <5 are different endpoints; check which outcome the vignette asks about."],"cue":"Ask “Is the limiting factor insufficient advancement, insufficient nightly use, or an airway phenotype that needs a different therapy?”","pages":{"id":6,"key":[101,102],"quick":[101,102],"detail":[103,112]},"count":80},{"id":7,"title":"Long-term oral appliance follow-up","short":"Long-term oral appliance follow-up","lens":"Detect relapse, dental change and appliance deterioration through structured longitudinal care.","sections":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Age, weight, alcohol, drugs and sleep","text":"Weight gain or loss, ageing, alcohol use, sedatives and new illness can alter OSA severity after an initially successful OAT course. Reassess the whole patient, not just whether the device still fits.","question_ids":[681,683,686,696,697,701,708,709,717,718,721,722],"examples":[{"id":681,"stem":"Three years after fitting, a patient gains 12 kg and develops recurrent snoring despite unchanged appliance setting. What should be assessed?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":272},{"id":683,"stem":"A long-term MAD user starts taking sedative medication and reports renewed snoring. Which factor is most pertinent to investigate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":272},{"id":686,"stem":"A previously successful patient begins using a sedating muscle relaxant at night. What should be documented?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":273},{"id":696,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. Which of the following statements about weight change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":277},{"id":697,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. The clinical team records the following finding: gain worsens OSA and may reduce effectiveness by altering airway anatomy and appliance fit; loss improves outcomes. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":277},{"id":701,"stem":"A patient now drinks substantial alcohol immediately before bed. What impact is most likely?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":278},{"id":708,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about sleep hygiene and quantity is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":281},{"id":709,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: good hygiene and adequate duration support success by stabilising architecture and reducing factors that worsen apnoea. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":281},{"id":717,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about medication change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":284},{"id":718,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. The clinical team records the following finding: drugs affecting the CNS or muscle tone (sedatives, hypnotics, opioids, muscle relaxants) may reduce airway patency or alter architecture, potentially reducing efficacy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":285},{"id":721,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. Which of the following statements about better long-term response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":286},{"id":722,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. The clinical team records the following finding: younger; less obese with smaller neck circumference (men); lower baseline AHI. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":287}],"count":12,"pdf_page":115},{"id":"7.2","title":"Longitudinal symptom documentation","text":"Record the original complaint, symptom scores when used, adherence, partner reports and any functional changes over time. Compare changes against the patient’s baseline rather than a generic “normal” pattern.","question_ids":[684,687,688,689,699,700,704,711,712],"examples":[{"id":684,"stem":"At baseline a patient recorded ESS and partner-rated snoring. Which approach best supports longitudinal comparison after years of OAT?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":273},{"id":687,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about evolution tracking is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":274},{"id":688,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: regular follow-ups, app-based tracking, and repeated administration of the same baseline questionnaires. Objective data integration — compliance-monitor data, CPAP usage logs, polysomnography, oximetry. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":274},{"id":689,"stem":"The patient says there is no snoring, but a partner witnesses pauses and choking again. What is the best response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":274},{"id":699,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. Which of the following statements about self-reported symptoms is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":278},{"id":700,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. The clinical team records the following finding: the patient's perception of severity and therapy effect: ESS, sleep diaries, mobile applications. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":278},{"id":704,"stem":"Baseline symptoms were recorded verbally only; no instrument was completed. How can future monitoring be improved?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":279},{"id":711,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about sleep-observer reports is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":282},{"id":712,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. The clinical team records the following finding: the bed partner or family on snoring intensity, apnoeas and restless sleep, offering \"objective-seeming\" data that complement self-report: partner observations, STOP-BANG items. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":282}],"count":9,"pdf_page":116},{"id":"7.3","title":"Treatment modification with progression","text":"Recurrent snoring or sleepiness, new cardiovascular morbidity, a large weight change or device failure should trigger dental and medical reassessment, often including repeat sleep testing after intervention.","question_ids":[682,690,691,698,702,703,710,713,714],"examples":[{"id":682,"stem":"A long-term MAD user previously achieved good OSA control. Following a 12% weight increase, the partner again notices apnoeic pauses. What is the most appropriate management?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":272},{"id":690,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about key considerations is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":275},{"id":691,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. The clinical team records the following finding: the optimal position is unique to each patient and determined by careful repeated titration. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":275},{"id":698,"stem":"A patient with worsening OSA cannot tolerate additional protrusion. What is the best plan?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":277},{"id":702,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. Which of the following statements about progression of the disease is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":279},{"id":703,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. The clinical team records the following finding: Sleep apnoea worsens over time, and the optimal advancement needed to keep the airway open may increase. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":279},{"id":710,"stem":"A patient's current treatment AHI is markedly higher than the historical value despite good nightly use. What does this most strongly justify?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":281},{"id":713,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. Which of the following statements about long-term dental change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":282},{"id":714,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. The clinical team records the following finding: Prolonged appliance use produces dentoskeletal change — reduced overbite and overjet, altered incisor position. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":283}],"count":9,"pdf_page":117},{"id":"7.4","title":"Annual device and clinical review","text":"Periodic appointments should review tooth and periodontal health, occlusion, TMJ, appliance retention, adjustment capacity, fractures, hygiene, adherence and the need for replacement. Follow-up intervals depend on risk and local standards.","question_ids":[685,692,693,694,695,705,706,707,715,716,719,720,723,724,725,726,727,728,729,730,731,732,733,734,735,736,737,738,739,740],"examples":[{"id":685,"stem":"At an annual review, a patient reports good nightly MAD use but has progressive overbite reduction. Which approach is best?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":273},{"id":692,"stem":"At an annual review, the device looks intact but the advancement mechanism slips during sleep. What is the priority?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":275},{"id":693,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. Which of the following statements about better response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":276},{"id":694,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. The clinical team records the following finding: younger, less obese, smaller neck (men), lower baseline AHI, positional OSA, NREM-predominant. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":276},{"id":695,"stem":"An old appliance has accumulated deposits, loss of retention and tooth migration. What should the review include?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":276},{"id":705,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about handling is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":280},{"id":706,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: always remove over a soft surface or a basin filled with water to prevent accidental drop damage; avoid hot water and direct sunlight, which warp the appliance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":280},{"id":707,"stem":"The patient asks why a dental appliance needs review once the AHI has been controlled. What is the best explanation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":280},{"id":715,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about poorer response is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":283},{"id":716,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: supine-predominant sleep during the study, REM-predominant OSA, weight gain during treatment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":284},{"id":719,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about occlusal contacts is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":285},{"id":720,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. The clinical team records the following finding: ensure the appliance does not interfere with the bite or cause premature contacts. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":286},{"id":723,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":287},{"id":724,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about weight is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":287},{"id":725,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about follow-up is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":288},{"id":726,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: every 6 months in year 1, at least annually thereafter; physician reassessment approximately every 5 years. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":288},{"id":727,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. Which of the following statements about surface integrity is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":288},{"id":728,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. The clinical team records the following finding: check for cracks, crazing or rough spots that could irritate the oral tissues. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":289},{"id":729,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about hardware functionality is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":289},{"id":730,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. The clinical team records the following finding: where metal components are present (clasps, hinges, screws), ensure they are free of corrosion and move smoothly without binding. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":290},{"id":731,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. Which of the following statements about material wear is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":290},{"id":732,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. The clinical team records the following finding: note discolouration, thinning or deformation of the acrylic or thermoplastic. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":291},{"id":733,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. Which of the following statements about retention is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":291},{"id":734,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. The clinical team records the following finding: gently tug to verify the device resists displacement during normal oral function. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":291},{"id":735,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about seating accuracy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":292},{"id":736,"stem":"A 61-year-old who has used a MAD for four years attends an annual dental review. The clinical team records the following finding: confirm the appliance fully engages the intended undercuts, or rests on the occlusal surfaces, without rocking. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":292},{"id":737,"stem":"A previously stable OSA patient reports recent weight gain and renewed snoring. Which of the following statements about cleaning is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":292},{"id":738,"stem":"A long-term appliance user asks whether routine reviews are still necessary. The clinical team records the following finding: rinse immediately after removal to wash away saliva and debris. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":293},{"id":739,"stem":"A patient returns after several years of nightly OAT with a new occlusal complaint. Which of the following statements about storage is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":293},{"id":740,"stem":"An established MAD user reports a change in alcohol intake and daytime symptoms. Which of the following statements about storage is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":294}],"count":30,"pdf_page":118}],"cases":[{"situation":"After three years of successful OAT, a patient gains 15 kg and reports loud snoring again.","decision":"Assess appliance fit/adherence and arrange medical reassessment with objective testing.","why":"Previous efficacy cannot be assumed to persist after major risk-factor change."},{"situation":"An annual review reveals asymptomatic overjet reduction compared with baseline photographs.","decision":"Discuss and document the change, review morning repositioning and appliance force/design, and agree on ongoing monitoring.","why":"Occlusal adaptation may be gradual even when the patient feels well."},{"situation":"The appliance has fractured at an adjustment mechanism but the patient continues to wear it.","decision":"Stop unsafe use, examine for injury and organise appropriate repair/replacement and review of disease control.","why":"A physically compromised device cannot be assumed to deliver the documented position."}],"pitfalls":["“Six-monthly for the first year, then yearly” may be an example schedule, not a substitute for risk-based clinical judgement.","The absence of symptoms does not eliminate the need to survey for dental and occlusal changes."],"cue":"Long-term success = acceptable disease control + sustained adherence + manageable effects on teeth, soft tissues and jaw function.","pages":{"id":7,"key":[113,114],"quick":[113,114],"detail":[115,121]},"count":60},{"id":8,"title":"Paediatric sleep-disordered breathing","short":"Paediatric sleep-disordered breathing","lens":"Recognise the different symptom profile, safeguard growth and avoid presenting orthodontic treatment as a universal OSA cure.","sections":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Paediatric prevalence","text":"Paediatric OSA occurs at lower respiratory-event indices than adult OSA. Prevalence estimates depend on age and scoring; do not import adult AHI thresholds into a child’s report.","question_ids":[769,774,788,791,802,805],"examples":[{"id":769,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: craniofacial morphology, tonsillar hypertrophy, altered neuromuscular tone, ventilatory control. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":304},{"id":774,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about the four contributing mechanisms is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":306},{"id":788,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: 3–12% (some sources cite 10–20%). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":312},{"id":791,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about habitual snoring is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":313},{"id":802,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: OSA 1–5% (typically 2–3%); habitual snoring 3–12% (some cite 10–20%). Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":318},{"id":805,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about Prevalence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":319}],"count":6,"pdf_page":124},{"id":"8.2","title":"Aetiology and pathophysiology","text":"Enlarged tonsils/adenoids, craniofacial restriction, obesity and neuromuscular factors may contribute. The dominant cause changes with age and comorbidity.","question_ids":[743,746,747,748],"examples":[{"id":743,"stem":"A 5-year-old snores loudly, breathes through the mouth and has enlarged palatine tonsils. Which anatomical factor is most commonly important at this age?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":295},{"id":746,"stem":"A preschool child develops obstructive breathing during sleep despite no obesity. The tonsils are markedly enlarged. Which aetiology is most typical?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":296},{"id":747,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about common preschool cause is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":297},{"id":748,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: Adenotonsillar hypertrophy is the predominant anatomical factor. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":297}],"count":4,"pdf_page":124},{"id":"8.3","title":"Signs and symptoms","text":"Children may present with inattention, behavioural or learning concerns, restless sleep, mouth breathing or growth concerns rather than classic adult daytime sleepiness.","question_ids":[741,742,749,750,751],"examples":[{"id":741,"stem":"A child with paediatric OSA is irritable and underperforming in school but not sleepy by adult measures. Why is the presentation plausible?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":295},{"id":742,"stem":"Parents bring a 7-year-old with habitual snoring, mouth breathing and disruptive behaviour at school. Which statement is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":295},{"id":749,"stem":"A six-year-old snores heavily and has hyperactivity and poor school concentration without daytime sleepiness. What should the dentist consider?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":297},{"id":750,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about paediatric daytime presentation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":298},{"id":751,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Attention, hyperactivity and behavioural or learning difficulties may predominate. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":298}],"count":5,"pdf_page":125},{"id":"8.4","title":"Insufficient sleep and development","text":"Insufficient sleep can mimic or amplify behavioural and cognitive complaints. Establish age-appropriate sleep opportunities and schedule, while assessing SDB when suggested by history.","question_ids":[753,754,764,772,777],"examples":[{"id":753,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about recommended duration is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":299},{"id":754,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: <4 months not specified (variation too large) · 4–12 months 12–16 h · 1–2 y 11–14 h · 3–5 y 10–13 h · 6–12 y 9–12 h · 13–18 y 8–10 h. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":299},{"id":764,"stem":"A 14-year-old sleeps 6.5 hours on school nights and catches up at weekends. Which issue should be assessed first?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":302},{"id":772,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: attention, behaviour and learning problems; increased risk of accidents, injuries, hypertension, obesity, diabetes and depression; in teenagers, increased self-harm, suicidal thoughts and suicide attempts. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":305},{"id":777,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about less than recommended is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":307}],"count":5,"pdf_page":126},{"id":"8.5","title":"Adolescent sleep and performance","text":"Adolescents commonly have a delayed circadian phase compounded by early school hours and device use. Short sleep does not prove OSA; assess timing, regularity and respiratory symptoms.","question_ids":[756,775,776,779,790,793,804,807,816,819,828,831,836,839],"examples":[{"id":756,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about mental health problems is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":300},{"id":775,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: Sleep pressure builds more slowly with time awake in adolescents. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":306},{"id":776,"stem":"An adolescent has late-night device use, delayed sleep onset and poor decisions during daytime. What is the most appropriate explanatory model?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":307},{"id":779,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about sleep–wake homeostasis is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":308},{"id":790,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: four mechanisms: media use directly displaces sleep; devices allow greater interaction with friends at night. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":313},{"id":793,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about electronic media is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":314},{"id":804,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about weekend catch-up is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":318},{"id":807,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about weekend catch-up is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":319},{"id":816,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Early adolescent development and pubertal onset are associated with a more evening-type circadian phase preference — the preferred timing of sleep, wake and daytime activity shifts later. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":324},{"id":819,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about circadian phase delay is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":325},{"id":828,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: early starts conflict with the delayed rhythm, producing chronic insufficient sleep. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":329},{"id":831,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about school start times is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":330},{"id":836,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: chronic medical illness; anxiety and stress; prescribed psychotropics. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":333},{"id":839,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about other causes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":334}],"count":14,"pdf_page":127},{"id":"8.6","title":"Paediatric screening","text":"A parental screening questionnaire or oral findings may identify high-risk children but cannot establish OSA severity. Prioritise appropriate paediatric medical assessment and testing.","question_ids":[744,757,759,767,778,781,792,795,806,809,818,821,830,833],"examples":[{"id":744,"stem":"Parents ask whether rapid maxillary expansion will reliably cure their child's PSG-confirmed OSA. Which counselling statement is best?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":296},{"id":757,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: retrognathia or micrognathia; subtle radiographic findings such as inferior hyoid position and reduced mandibular or maxillary length. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":300},{"id":759,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about jaw size and position is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":301},{"id":767,"stem":"A 7-year-old has loud snoring, mouth breathing and tonsils occupying more than half of the oropharyngeal space. What is the most useful action?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":303},{"id":778,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: use the modified Mallampati, Friedman tongue classification, or the Brodsky scale. Hypertrophy obstructing >50% of the airway warrants further evaluation — but tonsil size does not correlate with severity. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":307},{"id":781,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about tonsils and adenoids is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":308},{"id":792,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: chronic allergies, asthma, recurrent ear or throat infections, and a family history of sleep apnoea or SDB. Obesity, prematurity and syndromic conditions increase risk. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":314},{"id":795,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about medical, dental and family history is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":315},{"id":806,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. The clinical team records the following finding: children with SDB often display hyperactivity, irritability and difficulty concentrating, closely resembling ADHD. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":319},{"id":809,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about cognitive, behavioural and developmental assessment is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":320},{"id":818,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: high-arched palate or narrow arches restricting the airway; narrow arches may indicate habitual mouth breathing. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":325},{"id":821,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about palate and arches is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":326},{"id":830,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: tongue tie, low posture, restricted movement; abnormal posture or tongue thrusting may affect patency and craniofacial development. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":330},{"id":833,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about tongue posture and function is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":331}],"count":14,"pdf_page":129},{"id":"8.7","title":"Treatment approaches","text":"Adenotonsillectomy is frequently first-line when adenotonsillar hypertrophy contributes. Some children have persistent disease and need further medical and sometimes orthodontic, weight-related or PAP approaches.","question_ids":[755,761,762,780,783,794,797,808,811,820,823,832,835,838,840],"examples":[{"id":755,"stem":"A five-year-old with large obstructive tonsils has PSG-confirmed OSA. What is generally first-line treatment?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":299},{"id":761,"stem":"A parent requests rapid maxillary expansion specifically to cure their child's OSA. What is an evidence-consistent response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":301},{"id":762,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":302},{"id":780,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. The clinical team records the following finding: Attempt to improve adherence by addressing adverse effects and using behaviour modification techniques; if the child remains non-compliant, use an alternative treatment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":308},{"id":783,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about requires objective assessment of adherence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":309},{"id":794,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: AHI is increased supine and lowest prone in children — but position is hard to maintain overnight. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":315},{"id":797,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about positional and other is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":316},{"id":808,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: obesity; high pre-operative AHI; age <3 or ≥7; nasal abnormality or craniofacial disproportion; family history of OSA; asthma. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":320},{"id":811,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about risk factors for failure is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":321},{"id":820,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. The clinical team records the following finding: submucous cleft palate is relative — a partial adenotonsillectomy may be performed. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":326},{"id":823,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about contraindication is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":327},{"id":832,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: leaves tissue covering the tonsillar fossa musculature. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":331},{"id":835,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about partial tonsillectomy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":332},{"id":838,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: Intranasal corticosteroid — only for mild OSA (AHI <5/h), not moderate or severe. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":334},{"id":840,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about medication is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":334}],"count":15,"pdf_page":136},{"id":"8.8","title":"Differences from adult SDB","text":"Paediatric scoring and management depend on age, clinical context and local sleep-laboratory methods. Fixed age transitions and adult severity bands should not be applied uncritically.","question_ids":[752,760,765,782,785,796,799,810,813,822,825],"examples":[{"id":752,"stem":"A child has an obstructive AHI of 6 per hour. Which severity category follows the notebook?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":298},{"id":760,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: Children: thorough clinical evaluation of behaviour and sleep patterns; PSG; imaging (radiographs, endoscopy) may identify anatomical obstruction; end-tidal CO2 is a standard PSG measurement. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":301},{"id":765,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about diagnosis is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":303},{"id":782,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: Children: AHI ≥1 abnormal; mild 1–5, moderate 5–10, severe ≥10. Adults: AHI ≥5 with symptoms or ≥15 regardless; mild 5–15, moderate 15–30, severe ≥30. Age boundary 12 years. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":309},{"id":785,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about thresholds is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":310},{"id":796,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about causes is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":315},{"id":799,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about causes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":316},{"id":810,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: Children: snoring or noisy breathing; restless sleep or unusual positions; frequent bed-wetting; daytime fatigue and attention difficulties; behavioural problems that may mimic ADHD; poor academic performance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":321},{"id":813,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about presentation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":322},{"id":822,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about treatment is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":326},{"id":825,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about treatment is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":327}],"count":11,"pdf_page":138},{"id":"8.9","title":"Underlying syndromes and conditions","text":"High-risk groups, including Down syndrome, craniofacial syndromes, obesity and neuromuscular disease, warrant lower threshold for specialist assessment because symptoms may not reflect severity.","question_ids":[745,758,763,768,770,784,787,798,801,812,815,824,827],"examples":[{"id":745,"stem":"A 4-year-old child with Down syndrome has no parent-reported sleep complaints. What is the most appropriate assessment strategy?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":296},{"id":758,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome appears symptom-free but is approaching four years of age. Which plan accords with the notebook?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":300},{"id":763,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. The clinical team records the following finding: only a weak correlation exists between parental perception of sleep problems and polysomnographic results, and 53.8% of children with OSA had no symptoms. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":302},{"id":768,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about clinical symptoms are unreliable is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":304},{"id":770,"stem":"A child with obesity and craniofacial syndrome remains symptomatic after adenotonsillectomy. What is the next principle?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":304},{"id":784,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: Prevalence by AHI threshold: >1 in 69%; >1.5 in 76%; >2 in 75%; >5 (moderate–severe) in 50%; >10 in 34%. Compare 1–5% in the general paediatric population. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":310},{"id":787,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about OSA is highly prevalent is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":311},{"id":798,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. The clinical team records the following finding: obesity, asthma, allergic rhinitis, GORD and neuromuscular disorders all contribute to obstruction, inflammation or decreased airway muscle tone. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":316},{"id":801,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. Which of the following statements about medical conditions is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":317},{"id":812,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: Down, Prader–Willi, Crouzon and cleft lip and palate often involve craniofacial abnormalities — midfacial hypoplasia, macroglossia, retrognathia — narrowing the airway. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":322},{"id":815,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about genetic syndromes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":323},{"id":824,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. The clinical team records the following finding: macroglossia, midface hypoplasia, overweight, poor muscle tone. Moderate–severe disease is more prevalent at younger ages. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":327},{"id":827,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about predisposing factors is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":328}],"count":13,"pdf_page":139},{"id":"8.10","title":"Referral and diagnostic pathways","text":"Paediatric referral involves the child’s physician/sleep team and often ENT, orthodontic and other specialists. If maxillary constriction coexists, expansion may be an adjunct in a selected child but its effect on OSA is uncertain and needs objective follow-up.","question_ids":[766,771,773,786,789,800,803,814,817,826,829,834,837],"examples":[{"id":766,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: age <3 or ≥7, obesity, high pre-op AHI, nasal/craniofacial abnormality, family history, asthma. Overnight admission if SpO2 nadir <80%, AHI ≥24/h or peak PCO2 ≥60 mmHg. Partial tonsillectomy regrowth 0.5– 16%. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":303},{"id":771,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. Which of the following statements about adenotonsillectomy failure risk is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":305},{"id":773,"stem":"A parent reports habitual snoring in a preschool child and asks whether a dental examination diagnoses OSA. What is correct?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":306},{"id":786,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: 4–12 mo 12–16 h · 1–2 y 11–14 h · 3–5 y 10–13 h · 6–12 y 9–12 h · 13–18 y 8–10 h. <8 h in adolescence → ~3× suicide-attempt risk; caffeine users 2× more likely to have difficulty sleeping. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":311},{"id":789,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about sleep duration is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":312},{"id":800,"stem":"Parents report that their 6-year-old snores and struggles with classroom attention. The clinical team records the following finding: for children with multiple risk factors or symptoms, for comprehensive evaluation which may include PSG or further imaging. For enlarged adenoids and tonsils, surgical removal is first-line. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":317},{"id":803,"stem":"A child with Down syndrome attends a routine dental appointment and has no reported sleepiness. Which of the following statements about sleep physician or ent is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":318},{"id":814,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: normal <1 · mild 1–5 · moderate 5–10 · severe ≥10. Obstructive AHI ≥1 is abnormal. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":323},{"id":817,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about paediatric AHI is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":324},{"id":826,"stem":"An 8-year-old with a narrow palate and restless sleep is seen for an orthodontic assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: 2 mild / 4 moderate / 6 severe; paediatric ESS ≥10; tonsillar obstruction >50% warrants evaluation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":328},{"id":829,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. Which of the following statements about psq threshold >0.33 (>8 of 22 items); fairest-6 is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":329},{"id":834,"stem":"A 12-year-old with behavioural changes and habitual snoring is referred for assessment. The clinical team records the following finding: clinical symptoms of SDB, especially before adenotonsillectomy in children under 2 years, in obesity, in craniofacial or neuromuscular disorders, in Down syndrome, or where symptoms persist after surgery. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":332},{"id":837,"stem":"Parents request advice for a child with large tonsils and nocturnal gasping. Which of the following statements about indications for PSG is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":333}],"count":13,"pdf_page":140}],"cases":[{"situation":"A child with Down syndrome has no reported snoring but significant craniofacial and muscular risk factors.","decision":"Support the recommended paediatric sleep screening/PSG pathway even without a convincing parental symptom report.","why":"Symptoms are an unreliable rule-out tool in some high-risk groups."},{"situation":"A child with narrow maxilla and PSG-confirmed OSA asks whether expansion guarantees cure.","decision":"Explain that orthodontic correction may be considered for a dentofacial indication as part of multidisciplinary management, with objective reassessment.","why":"The 2024 ATS recommendation is conditional with very-low-certainty evidence."},{"situation":"A schoolchild remains symptomatic after adenotonsillectomy.","decision":"Seek sleep-physician reassessment for residual OSA and assessment of additional anatomical or non-anatomical factors.","why":"Surgery does not cure every child and persistent disease has several treatment paths."}],"pitfalls":["Do not state that rapid maxillary expansion universally cures OSA or that a single post-treatment percentage predicts the child’s outcome.","Children are not simply “small adults”; symptoms and PSG scoring require paediatric context."],"cue":"Paediatric SBA priorities: identify a high-risk child, select the right referral, and avoid overclaiming the respiratory benefit of dental expansion.","pages":{"id":8,"key":[122,123],"quick":[122,123],"detail":[124,143]},"count":100},{"id":9,"title":"Best-practice, documentation and ethical responsibilities","short":"Best practice, records and professional scope","lens":"Know who diagnoses, what must be documented, and how to protect the patient when care spans professions and jurisdictions.","sections":[{"id":"9.1","title":"Physician diagnosis and portable testing","text":"Under the 2025 AADSM standards, a qualified dentist may order/administer an HSAT where local law permits, but an authorised medical provider is responsible for diagnosing OSA. A dental screening result or titration signal is not a self-standing diagnosis.","question_ids":[841,842,856,857,861,868,869,877,878,885,886,893,894],"examples":[{"id":841,"stem":"A patient asks whether a dentist can declare 'no OSA' after a home oximetry test. What is the best scope-of-practice response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":335},{"id":842,"stem":"An adult has an elevated STOP-BANG score and wants a dentist to certify a diagnosis of OSA. Which is the best response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":335},{"id":856,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. Which of the following statements about special indications is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":340},{"id":857,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. The clinical team records the following finding: where in-laboratory PSG is impossible because of immobility, safety or critical illness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":340},{"id":861,"stem":"A clinician uses an HSAT in a patient with suspected narcolepsy and significant heart failure. Why is this problematic?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":341},{"id":868,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about accurate, with a low failure rate, and attended by technical staff is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":344},{"id":869,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: but expensive and technically complex. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":344},{"id":877,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. Which of the following statements about portable monitoring is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":347},{"id":878,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: less expensive and quicker, and can be as accurate as PSG in selected populations. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":348},{"id":885,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. Which of the following statements about in-laboratory PSG is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":351},{"id":886,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: accurate with a low failure rate, attended by technical staff; expensive and technically complex. Also the standard for narcolepsy and central sleep apnoea. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":352},{"id":893,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about indication is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":355},{"id":894,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: may be used as an alternative to PSG in patients with a high pre-test probability of moderate-to-severe OSA. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":356}],"count":13,"pdf_page":146},{"id":"9.2","title":"Clinical records and communication","text":"Maintain the diagnostic report, symptom history, examination, baseline dental records, rationale for appliance selection, signed consent, construction bite, titration log, follow-up tests, adverse-event management and physician communications.","question_ids":[843,846,847,848,849,859,860,864,871,872,879,880,887,888,895,896],"examples":[{"id":843,"stem":"A patient disputes a course of OAT because no baseline dental records were stored. Which omission most weakens the clinic's ability to assess treatment-related bite change?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":335},{"id":846,"stem":"An oral appliance was fitted without recording pretreatment overjet, overbite or models. What is the key risk?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":336},{"id":847,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about treatment planning is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":337},{"id":848,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: comprehensive plans setting out rationale, appliance choice, adjustments, titration and patient education, with any shared decision-making discussions about options and intolerances recorded. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":337},{"id":849,"stem":"A dentist changes advancement settings but does not inform the sleep physician. Which best-practice principle is breached?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":337},{"id":859,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. Which of the following statements about informed consent is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":341},{"id":860,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: complete signed forms explaining purpose, risks, benefits, alternatives and patient rights. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":341},{"id":864,"stem":"The patient disputes whether appliance-related tooth movement was discussed. Which record most directly supports a proper process?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":342},{"id":871,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. Which of the following statements about ongoing clinical notes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":345},{"id":872,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: patient assessments, symptoms, progress, adherence and response, including evaluations and observations from both physicians and qualified dentists. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":345},{"id":879,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. Which of the following statements about baseline sleep study data is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":348},{"id":880,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: the PSG or home test results with detailed sleep parameters, AHI, architecture and arousals, properly stored for treatment reference and follow-up comparison. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":349},{"id":887,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about interprofessional communication is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":352},{"id":888,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: records of communication between sleep physicians, dentists and others, ensuring coordinated care with shared assessment, adjustments and follow-up, documented with patient permission. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":353},{"id":895,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. Which of the following statements about delivery documentation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":356},{"id":896,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. The clinical team records the following finding: written home care instructions, delivery form and warranty, signed and dated by patient and team member, one copy to the patient and one retained. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":357}],"count":16,"pdf_page":148},{"id":"9.3","title":"Billing and payer codes","text":"Use current local payer and professional coding rules; diagnostic testing, dental device supply and follow-up may have distinct requirements. Do not copy old Australian or other country-specific item numbers into a different jurisdiction.","question_ids":[844,850,851,852,862,863,873,874,881,882,889,890,897,898],"examples":[{"id":844,"stem":"A dentist practising across two countries is asked to use a particular reimbursement item number for OAT. What is the safest response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":336},{"id":850,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. Which of the following statements about consult authoritative sources is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":338},{"id":851,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: the relevant government health department or insurer website — for current guidance rather than relying on memory or on colleagues. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":338},{"id":852,"stem":"A practice sees patients funded through a veterans' scheme and proposes using private insurance codes interchangeably. What is appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":338},{"id":862,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. Which of the following statements about separate conditions is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":342},{"id":863,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. The clinical team records the following finding: For veterans with both DVA and other insurance, clearly separate discussion and documentation of unrelated conditions when billing multiple insurers. Best practice for both. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":342},{"id":873,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. Which of the following statements about identify the correct codes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":346},{"id":874,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. The clinical team records the following finding: Use item codes and descriptions specific to the service provided and to the patient's in- surer. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":346},{"id":881,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about use dva-specific codes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":349},{"id":882,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: Veterans' affairs schemes maintain their own set of codes, which can differ from those used for the general public health insurer or for private insurance. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":350},{"id":889,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. Which of the following statements about ethical dimension is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":353},{"id":890,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. The clinical team records the following finding: billing and coding fall explicitly within the professionalism competencies of a qualified dentist. Appliance selection must never be driven by what an insurer will reimburse. 9.4 Ethics. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":354},{"id":897,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. Which of the following statements about private billing is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":357},{"id":898,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. The clinical team records the following finding: identify codes specific to the service and the insurer; maintain clear, separate consultation notes differentiating insurers and conditions. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":358}],"count":14,"pdf_page":149},{"id":"9.4","title":"Ethics and professional competence","text":"Ethical practice includes autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, veracity, confidentiality and competence. Clinical decisions must not be controlled by laboratory incentives, insurance targets or unsubstantiated efficacy claims.","question_ids":[845,853,854,855,858,865,866,867,870,875,876,883,884,891,892,899,900],"examples":[{"id":845,"stem":"A clinic is offered a financial incentive for exclusively fitting one oral appliance brand, regardless of individual patient needs. Which ethical principle is most directly at risk?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":336},{"id":853,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. Which of the following statements about pm minimum is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":339},{"id":854,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. The clinical team records the following finding: airflow + respiratory effort + blood oxygenation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":339},{"id":855,"stem":"A dentist with a commercial relationship to an appliance supplier recommends it without comparison. What ethical principle is at stake?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":339},{"id":858,"stem":"A patient declines OAT after learning about permanent bite changes. What should the clinician do?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":340},{"id":865,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about beneficence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":343},{"id":866,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: promote well-being and provide high-quality care improving oral and overall health. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":343},{"id":867,"stem":"A patient with financial barriers cannot access a custom appliance. Which ethical principle requires attention to equitable access?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":343},{"id":870,"stem":"A dentist accurately discloses uncertainty about the appliance's expected success and limitations of research. Which principle is best illustrated?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":344},{"id":875,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. Which of the following statements about scope of competence is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":347},{"id":876,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. The clinical team records the following finding: treat only within your qualifications and expertise; deliver care within the qualified dentist's competence. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":347},{"id":883,"stem":"A dentist and sleep physician are defining responsibilities for an OAT referral pathway. Which of the following statements about autonomy is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":350},{"id":884,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. The clinical team records the following finding: patients have the right to make informed decisions; in practice, genuine informed consent requiring clear understanding of purpose, risks, benefits and alternatives. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":351},{"id":891,"stem":"A dental clinic is auditing its written records for an oral appliance therapy service. Which of the following statements about professionalism is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":354},{"id":892,"stem":"An adult arrives with an elevated STOP-BANG score but no physician diagnosis of OSA. The clinical team records the following finding: ethical principles including billing and coding; accountability and team training with adherence to applicable laws. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":355},{"id":899,"stem":"A patient asks a dentist to provide a diagnosis on the basis of an overnight oximetry report. Which of the following statements about non-maleficence (do no harm) is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":358},{"id":900,"stem":"A clinic considers an industry incentive linked to its choice of oral appliance. The clinical team records the following finding: use evidence-based practices, stay updated, and provide treatment only within established competence. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":359}],"count":17,"pdf_page":150}],"cases":[{"situation":"A dentist in a jurisdiction permitting HSAT obtains a positive home result.","decision":"Send findings for medical interpretation/diagnosis and coordinate treatment decisions; follow applicable local law.","why":"Permitted ordering does not by itself confer diagnostic authority."},{"situation":"A payer asks the clinic to invoice a different device code to obtain reimbursement.","decision":"Use accurate documentation and the applicable valid code, and explain limitations to the patient.","why":"False coding is not justified by a clinically appropriate intention."},{"situation":"The sleep physician has not returned objective follow-up results after OAT titration.","decision":"Request the results and document communication before declaring verified disease control.","why":"A dentist should not silently substitute symptom improvement for the missing medical outcome assessment."}],"pitfalls":["The notebook’s older categorical wording about dentists and HSAT needs a jurisdiction-specific qualification; diagnosis remains medical under the cited 2025 standards.","A signed consent form is evidence of documentation, not proof that the patient understood material risks."],"cue":"For scope-of-practice SBAs, identify the responsible clinician for screening, HSAT logistics, diagnosis, dental treatment and efficacy verification separately.","pages":{"id":9,"key":[144,145],"quick":[144,145],"detail":[146,153]},"count":60},{"id":10,"title":"Informed consent and management of adverse effects","short":"Consent, side effects and treatment decisions","lens":"Disclose expected risks, distinguish adaptation from harm, and use proportional decisions when adverse effects threaten treatment.","sections":[{"id":"10.1","title":"Valid informed consent","text":"Valid informed consent is a two-way process covering diagnosis, uncertainty, expected benefits, likely limitations, alternative treatment (including no therapy) and material harms. Document the patient’s questions and choices.","question_ids":[901,902,922,923,927],"examples":[{"id":901,"stem":"Before a MAD is fitted, a patient signs a form but cannot explain any long-term side effects. What is the best response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":360},{"id":902,"stem":"Before starting OAT, a patient signs a form but says that long-term bite changes and alternative treatments were never explained. Which is the best conclusion?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":360},{"id":922,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about three tests of valid consent is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":367},{"id":923,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: capacity, voluntariness (free of undue influence) and comprehension — verified, not assumed, for example by asking patients to repeat back what they heard. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":367},{"id":927,"stem":"A patient has capacity and freely consents but does not know that CPAP and no treatment are alternatives. What is missing?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":368}],"count":5,"pdf_page":156},{"id":"10.2","title":"Short/long-term side effects","text":"Dry mouth, salivation changes, dental tenderness and transient muscular symptoms may arise early. Long-term overjet, overbite and occlusal changes are sufficiently relevant to discuss before treatment rather than only after they occur.","question_ids":[906,907,908,925,926,937,938,947,948,961,962,981,982,989,990,995,996,999],"examples":[{"id":906,"stem":"A person develops reduced overjet after five years of nightly MAD use. How should this be framed?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":361},{"id":907,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about Mechanism is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":362},{"id":908,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about Mechanism is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":362},{"id":925,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about CPAP is not innocent is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":368},{"id":926,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: CPAP also causes retroclination of the upper incisors, from posteriorly directed forces on the anterior maxilla by a tightly fitted nasal mask, and long-term use of both CPAP and appliances produces significant dental…. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":368},{"id":937,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about direction of force is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":372},{"id":938,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: appliances produce distally directed forces on the upper dentition and forwardly directed forces on the lower teeth. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":373},{"id":947,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about where noticed, symptoms of bite change are is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":377},{"id":948,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: gaps and food impaction; occasional lisping when talking; and — rarely — difficulty chewing on the back teeth or tearing food with the front teeth. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":378},{"id":961,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about position of professional bodies is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":383},{"id":962,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: for most people the change in bite is not annoying, is not a risk to oral health, and does not require further dental treatment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":384},{"id":981,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about what patients actually experience is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":391},{"id":982,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: Mostly changes are minor and unnoticed; only a minority notice. Most are unaware, untroubled, and do not want to stop. Where noticed: gaps and food impaction; occasional lisping; rarely difficulty chewing on. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":392},{"id":989,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about causes is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":395},{"id":990,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about causes is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":395},{"id":995,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about frequency is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":397},{"id":996,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about frequency is most accurate?","kind":"Focused knowledge SBA","pdf_page":397},{"id":999,"stem":"A 58-year-old has used a mandibular advancement appliance nightly for seven years. Compared with baseline, overjet has reduced from 3 mm to 1 mm and posterior contacts have altered. Which is the most likely explanation?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":398}],"count":18,"pdf_page":159},{"id":"10.3","title":"Craniofacial and TMJ effects","text":"Mandibular advancement can affect teeth, periodontal loading, jaw muscles and TMJ function. Record baseline occlusion, joint symptoms and protrusive range to distinguish treatment-related change from pre-existing findings.","question_ids":[903,910,911,921,928,929,939,940],"examples":[{"id":903,"stem":"After years of nightly mandibular advancement, a patient has reduced overjet and fewer posterior contacts compared with baseline. Which is the most likely contributor?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":360},{"id":910,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about soft tissues is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":363},{"id":911,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: hypersalivation or dry mouth; the intended airway enlargement by advancing the mandible and repositioning the tongue. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":363},{"id":921,"stem":"A patient with minimal baseline overjet develops an anterior crossbite during prolonged therapy. Why were they vulnerable?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":366},{"id":928,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about corollary is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":369},{"id":929,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: an appliance is already effective at a less protrusive position in mild and moderate OSA, and therefore causes less severe dental side effects. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":369},{"id":939,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about factors influencing severity is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":373},{"id":940,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: risk increases with greater advancement, with levels exceeding 50% of maximum often correlating with significantly higher risk. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":374}],"count":8,"pdf_page":161},{"id":"10.4","title":"Morning guide and exercises","text":"Morning repositioning devices and exercises may help re-establish habitual bite contact after nightly wear, but do not guarantee prevention or complete reversal of long-term tooth movement.","question_ids":[904,909,913,914,931,932,941,942,949,950,955,956,963,964,969,970,975,976,983,984],"examples":[{"id":904,"stem":"A MAD user describes a transient altered bite every morning. Which adjunctive approach may be recommended after examination?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":361},{"id":909,"stem":"A MAD user wakes with a temporary posterior open bite. Which preventive strategy appears in the notes?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":362},{"id":913,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about exercises increase contact between the teeth is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":364},{"id":914,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: good in the short term, with limited long-term evidence. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":364},{"id":931,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about perform slowly and gently is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":370},{"id":932,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: opening the mouth wide, moving the jaw left and right, resting the chin on a fist to let the jaw settle, and curling the tongue back to find the retruded position. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":370},{"id":941,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about two fabrication types is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":374},{"id":942,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: those using thermoacrylic beads, and those using a prefabricated sheet (AM aligner) placed in hot water, moulded around the teeth, with the patient asked to bite in centric occlusion. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":375},{"id":949,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about exercises the jaw muscles is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":378},{"id":950,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: applying pressure exercises and stretches the muscles of mastication, which are tight after a night of wear. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":379},{"id":955,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about realigns the bite is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":381},{"id":956,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: custom-made to fit the patient's upper and lower teeth, guiding them back to their original maximum intercuspation. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":381},{"id":963,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about monitors progress is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":384},{"id":964,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: the patient uses it each morning to check jaw alignment; if it does not fit, they should contact the practice. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":385},{"id":969,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about positions the condyles is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":386},{"id":970,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: assists in repositioning the condyles to their pre-treatment alignment, reducing strain and discomfort. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":387},{"id":975,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about wear for 15–20 minutes in the morning after waking is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":389},{"id":976,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: some protocols specify around 10 minutes. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":390},{"id":983,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about how it works is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":392},{"id":984,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: realigns the bite toward original maximum intercuspation; exercises and stretches the muscles of mastication, tight after a night of wear. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":393}],"count":20,"pdf_page":162},{"id":"10.5","title":"Managing side effects","text":"Manage mild symptoms with fit adjustment, slower titration, oral care and follow-up; investigate progressive joint pain, tooth mobility, tissue injury or significant occlusal change promptly. Tailor interventions to severity and patient preference.","question_ids":[905,912,915,916,917,930,933,934,943,944,951,952,957,958,965,966,971,972,977,978,985,986,991,992,997,998,1000],"examples":[{"id":905,"stem":"After several weeks of MAD use, a patient develops persistent TMJ pain and restricted opening. Which action is best?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":361},{"id":912,"stem":"A patient develops mild transient salivation soon after appliance insertion. What is the usual first response?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":363},{"id":915,"stem":"Persistent severe unilateral jaw pain develops during titration. What action is most appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":364},{"id":916,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about minor bite change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":365},{"id":917,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: mechanical forces causing gradual shifts. → jaw exercises, passive exercises, morning occlusal guide; adjust the appliance or settings. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":365},{"id":930,"stem":"A device repeatedly traumatizes the gingiva while the patient's AHI is improved. What is appropriate?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":369},{"id":933,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about regular follow-up is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":370},{"id":934,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: every 6 months to a year, to evaluate for and manage side effects appropriately, with documentation of presence, management and resolution, and communication to the general dentist and medical provider. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":371},{"id":943,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about transient morning jaw pain is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":375},{"id":944,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: muscles adjusting to the forward mandible. → jaw exercises, massage, heat; morning occlusal guide. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":376},{"id":951,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about palliative care is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":379},{"id":952,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: supportive care to manage discomfort, especially during the initial adaptation period. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":380},{"id":957,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about excessive salivation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":381},{"id":958,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: the appliance itself. → towel on the pillow; reassurance, resolving in 2–3 weeks. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":382},{"id":965,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about patient education is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":385},{"id":966,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: reassure that symptoms are likely temporary and manageable. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":385},{"id":971,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about soft tissue irritation is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":387},{"id":972,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: screw mechanism or rough surface rubbing tongue or cheek; cheek or lip biting from bite change. → polish; discontinue until healed; change appliance; orthodontic wax. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":388},{"id":977,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about monitoring and adjustment is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":390},{"id":978,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. The clinical team records the following finding: a qualified dentist is essential for monitoring side effects and making necessary adjustments to the appliance or plan. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":390},{"id":985,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about unilateral jaw pain is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":393},{"id":986,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: prone (stomach) sleeping; appliance not seating; imbalance in appliance or midlines. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":394},{"id":991,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about dry mouth is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":396},{"id":992,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: poor nasal breathing → mouth breathing; vertical dimension too large making lip seal difficult. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":396},{"id":997,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. Which of the following statements about bilateral jaw pain is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":398},{"id":998,"stem":"A MAD user develops bilateral jaw pain and limited opening after the appliance is advanced beyond the previously comfortable position. Which factor is the most likely contributor?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":398},{"id":1000,"stem":"A patient develops persistent TMJ tenderness and restricted opening after increasing mandibular protrusion from 5 mm to 7 mm. Snoring is improved, but oral function is now impaired. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":399}],"count":27,"pdf_page":163},{"id":"10.6","title":"Suspending or abandoning treatment","text":"Pause or stop OAT for serious harm, unsuitable dental support or unacceptable persistent symptoms; then coordinate alternative OSA treatment and verification to avoid leaving significant disease untreated.","question_ids":[918,919,920,924,935,936,945,946,953,954,959,960,967,968,973,974,979,980,987,988,993,994],"examples":[{"id":918,"stem":"A patient cannot tolerate further protrusion but retains substantial residual OSA. What should be discussed?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":365},{"id":919,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about patient-specific factors is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":366},{"id":920,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: overall health, tolerance for side effects and commitment to therapy. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":366},{"id":924,"stem":"After an appliance is discontinued, minor occlusal change is detected. What is the preferred initial approach in the notebook?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":367},{"id":935,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about device and settings is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":371},{"id":936,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: efficacy and side effects depend on appliance type, degree of protrusion and other design settings, so modification may be preferable to abandonment. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":372},{"id":945,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. Which of the following statements about where treatment stops is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":376},{"id":946,"stem":"An adult with new TMJ discomfort returns after increasing mandibular protrusion. The clinical team records the following finding: notify the medical provider, and refer non-compliant patients back to consider alternatives, since untreated or subtherapeutic OSA carries long-term risk. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":377},{"id":953,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about discuss with the patient and record the discussion is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":380},{"id":954,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: the conversation is only protective if documented. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":380},{"id":959,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about regular follow-up is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":382},{"id":960,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: monitoring by qualified dentists is necessary to survey for dental side effects and occlusal change, and is essential for maximising adherence and effectiveness. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":383},{"id":967,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about regular reviews is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":386},{"id":968,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: at least 6-monthly for the first year, then annually. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":386},{"id":973,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. Which of the following statements about occlusal change the patient finds unacceptable is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":388},{"id":974,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. The clinical team records the following finding: treatment should be discontinued only if the patient accepts another form of therapy from their attending physician, or understands the risks of discontinuation and their physician is informed. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":389},{"id":979,"stem":"A patient starting long-term MAD therapy asks about bite change and jaw discomfort. Which of the following statements about leave it alone within the first 12–18 months is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":391},{"id":980,"stem":"At an annual OAT review, a patient notices a reduction in overjet and altered occlusal contacts. The clinical team records the following finding: most will self-correct. This is why it is important to review vulnerable patients early, so that this option remains available. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":391},{"id":987,"stem":"A dentist is discussing material risks and reasonable alternatives before appliance delivery. Which of the following statements about occlusal change is most accurate?","kind":"Applied clinical SBA","pdf_page":394},{"id":988,"stem":"A patient using a MAD reports persistent morning occlusal changes and reduced comfort. The clinical team records the following finding: OJ ~1.9 mm, OB ~2.3 mm (average ~2.5 mm); ~⅓ exceed 1 mm; at 17 y median −1.1 / −1.6 mm; ~80% of 7-year nightly users; 85% detectable on models and cephalograms; 13.3% by 2 years then constant. Which concept best explains this observation?","kind":"Clinical interpretation SBA","pdf_page":395},{"id":993,"stem":"A patient experiences a persistent new posterior open bite after several years of oral appliance therapy. The appliance remains effective for OSA and the patient wishes to continue. Which management principle is best?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":396},{"id":994,"stem":"A patient has persistent severe jaw pain despite reduction of mandibular advancement, a temporary treatment pause and appropriate conservative measures. Which is the best next management decision?","kind":"Clinical decision-making SBA","pdf_page":397}],"count":22,"pdf_page":165}],"cases":[{"situation":"A patient has developed an increasing posterior open bite two years after successful OAT.","decision":"Compare baseline records, explain the observed change, evaluate the device and morning guide, and jointly consider modifications and monitoring.","why":"Dental changes can occur over time even with good respiratory control."},{"situation":"A patient develops severe, worsening TMJ pain after aggressive titration.","decision":"Stop further advancement and consider temporary suspension, TMJ assessment and physician-coordinated alternative therapy.","why":"Continuing the same titration despite escalating pain is not an acceptable risk-benefit response."},{"situation":"A patient signs a printed consent form but says they did not understand that their bite might change permanently.","decision":"Revisit the informed-consent discussion, explain risks and alternatives and document their new decision before proceeding.","why":"Consent is informed understanding, not simply possession of a signature."}],"pitfalls":["Do not promise that a morning aligner completely prevents occlusal changes or that every minor early symptom mandates permanent cessation.","Do not abandon airway management when discontinuing OAT; the patient still needs an appropriate OSA care pathway."],"cue":"A defensible SBA answer separates the dental adverse effect, its severity, the patient’s informed preference and the continuing need to treat OSA.","pages":{"id":10,"key":[154,155],"quick":[154,155],"detail":[156,177]},"count":100}];
